{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780803012846,"sessionID":"ses_15fdf386fffe9fkXvMGWNEHLbs","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_6PGWBzuZFYlsB5PdbWi6Fa0t","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/ai/conventions/code-quality.md","offset":261,"limit":120},"output":"<path>/home/nes/ai/conventions/code-quality.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n261: \n262: - LOW: `agents/provider-quota-filter.md` is declared with `role: intrinsic-surface`, `Domain: quota_state`, and `Owns:` containing `provider_quotas`, `provider_quota_windows`, `exhausted_at`, `resets_at`, `filtered_indices`, and `clear_exhausted`. The component covers 1 domain and all external references are subordinate to the declared quota-state symbols and operations.\n263: - HIGH: `agents/provider-quota-filter.md` declares `role: intrinsic-surface` for `Domain: quota_state` but also reads routing-log internals or writes billing records outside `Owns:`. The intrinsic-surface declaration does not apply as LOW because non-subordinate references are reached.\n264: \n265: \n266: ## Disposition policy\n267: \n268: Only LOW passes pipeline-callable code-quality gates. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` block advance, trigger remediation/revise, and require a rerun from current evidence. Neither severity is ever a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user choice, or a \"stable\" allow-advance state for code-quality / risk-gate / prototype-risk verdicts.\n269: \n270: For current, well-formed semantic `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` findings, `~/ai/conventions/decomposition-strategies.md` selects remediation, MOVE, split, or decomposition handling. Neither severity becomes a residual or user-disposition state through that selector.\n271: \n272: This rule is the ACR-156 parent regression disposition and is enforced across all four canonical surfaces: `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`, `~/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md`, this convention, and `~/ai/workflows/code-quality.md`.\n273: \n274: ### Bootstrap exception\n275: \n276: A Work Unit whose primary deliverable is itself a change to the code-quality metric, convention, verifier, or auditor surface may, narrowly, advance past a pipeline-callable code-quality gate that scores `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` on the intrinsic lockstep elements that the WU's own change is rewriting. The exception applies only when ALL of the following conditions are met:\n277: \n278: 1. The WU's primary deliverable fixes or extends the code-quality metric or rule currently scoring the WU non-LOW.\n279: 2. The non-LOW finding is on intrinsic lockstep elements required by the metric / convention / verifier / auditor change itself, not on collateral product code.\n280: 3. The post-merge codebase satisfies the new rule under the new metric — i.e. once the WU lands, the same audit on the new tree returns LOW.\n281: 4. The exception is declared in Phase 3 of `~/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md` and ratified in the Phase 4 join manifest as a `bootstrap-exception` row marked `RATIFIED`, with a corresponding `### <ticket-id> — Bootstrap exception ratification` entry in `${worktree_path}/DECISIONS.md` citing this subsection.\n282: \n283: This is the ONLY local carve-out from the LOW-only rule above. Ordinary residual acceptance, `NEEDS_INPUT` user choice, stable-allow-advance, or any manager-flavor / root / DECISIONS-only acceptance of a non-LOW pipeline-callable code-quality verdict remains forbidden under `~/ai/conventions/workflow-execution-violations.md` § `Named anti-pattern: Non-LOW gate residual acceptance`. Missing the Phase 3 declaration, missing the DECISIONS ratification entry, missing the citation to this subsection, or missing the Phase 4 join-manifest `bootstrap-exception` row marked `RATIFIED` falls through to the LOW-only blocking rule above.\n284: \n285: ### Oscillation signals WU-too-large\n286: \n287: After two consecutive rounds that do not converge to `LOW`, the Work Unit is too large for the current grain and must decompose instead of attempting a third remediation pass. Decompose is autonomous, not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated; the caller records the oscillation evidence and opens the smaller WU path required by the owning workflow.\n288: \n289: After this two-round non-convergence trigger fires, `~/ai/conventions/decomposition-strategies.md` names the smaller-WU shape and any follow-up-ticket handoff evidence. It does not make decompose `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated.\n290: \n291: ## Numerical thresholds\n292: \n293: These are starting calibration points for downstream auditors A4 / A5 / A6 (NES-140 / NES-141 / NES-148). Those auditors may refine measurement details, but they should treat these thresholds as the initial shared baseline.\n294: \n295: | Metric | LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH |\n296: |---|---|---|---|\n297: | `Nesting depth` | 0-1 | n/a | >= 2 |\n298: | `Function categories per function` | 1 | n/a | >= 2 |\n299: | `Cohesion by classifications touched` | actual classifications are a subset of the declared role set (file-local, path default, or component-level declared roles in a Phase 6a contract); for components and files without any declared roles, exactly 1 classification | n/a | actual classifications exceed the declared role set or include classifications outside the declared role set; for components and files without any declared roles, 2 or more classifications |\n300: | `Coupling by distinct external symbols/modules referenced` | 0-2 | 3-5 | >= 6 |\n301: \n302: The first two rows are hard code-shape thresholds in this convention. The cohesion and coupling rows are review calibration: they identify where a function is likely doing too many kinds of work or depending on too many external surfaces.\n303: \n304: ## Failure modes\n305: \n306: - `multi-classifier function`\n307: - `deep-nest violator`\n308: - `inline-function carrier`\n309: - `duplicate-responsibility carrier`\n310: - `uncontrolled-source coupler`\n311: \n312: ## What this convention does not enforce\n313: \n314: This convention is policy, not enforcement. Auditors and future WUs enforce it. No linter, CI gate, pre-commit hook, workflow gate, language-specific style configuration, or runtime checker is established by this file.\n315: \n316: It also does not replace project-local architecture rules. A project may have stricter framework, package, API, or naming rules; those rules layer on top of this convention when they do not conflict with it.\n317: \n318: ## Cross-references\n319: \n320: - `~/ai/conventions/decomposition-strategies.md` - selector for remediation, MOVE, split, or decomposition after existing ownership, LOW-only, bootstrap, and oscillation signals fire.\n321: - `~/ai/workflows/code-quality.md` - composite dispatch workflow for existing A1 auditors; this is an operational pointer only and does not change this convention's rule text, failure modes, numerical thresholds, or policy-only status.\n322: - `~/ai/workflows/code-quality.md` § `Stop Conditions And Escalation` - separates standalone advisory use from pipeline-callable blocking use; pipeline-callable blocking enforces this disposition policy, including LOW-only passage and autonomous decomposition after oscillation.\n323: - `~/ai/agents/function-classification-auditor.md` - A5 / NES-141 enforcement surface for the `Function categories per function` threshold and `multi-classifier function` findings; this is a forward pointer only and does not change this convention's categories, rule, failure modes, thresholds, or policy-only status.\n324: - `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Scoring axes - duplicate-system count, change-path entropy, and risk scoring mechanics.\n325: - `~/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md` § Step 2.5.4 and § Phase 3 - duplicate-systems inventory plus cascade/consolidate/accept handling.\n326: - `~/ai/conventions/agent-questions-and-session-graph.md` § Pull-vs-Push Policy - terminology disambiguation only.\n327: - `~/ai/agents/push-pull-auditor.md` - A4 / NES-140 enforcement surface for the `Push-vs-pull system coupling` rule and `uncontrolled-source coupler` findings; this is a forward pointer only and does not change this convention's rule text, failure modes, numerical thresholds, or policy-only status.\n328: - `~/ai/conventions/workflow-execution-violations.md` § `Named anti-pattern: Non-LOW gate residual acceptance` - the forbidden baseline this convention's `### Bootstrap exception` is narrowly carved from; manager / root / DECISIONS-only acceptance of a non-LOW pipeline-callable code-quality verdict remains a blocking gate/termination violation outside the four-condition path declared and ratified per § `Bootstrap exception`.\n\n(End of file - total 328 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\n- LOW: `agents/provider-quota-filter.md` is declared with `role: intrinsic-surface`, `Domain: quota_state`, and `Owns:` containing `provider_quotas`, `provider_quota_windows`, `exhausted_at`, `resets_at`, `filtered_indices`, and `clear_exhausted`. The component covers 1 domain and all external references are subordinate to the declared quota-state symbols and operations.\n- HIGH: `agents/provider-quota-filter.md` declares `role: intrinsic-surface` for `Domain: quota_state` but also reads routing-log internals or writes billing records outside `Owns:`. The intrinsic-surface declaration does not apply as LOW because non-subordinate references are reached.\n\n\n## Disposition policy\n\nOnly LOW passes pipeline-callable code-quality gates. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` block advance, trigger remediation/revise, and require a rerun from current evidence. Neither severity is ever a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user choice, or a \"stable\" allow-advance state for code-quality / risk-gate / prototype-risk verdicts.\n\nFor current, well-formed semantic `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` findings, `~/ai/conventions/decomposition-strategies.md` selects remediation, MOVE, split, or decomposition handling. Neither severity becomes a residual or user-disposition state through that selector.\n\nThis rule is the ACR-156 parent regression disposition and is enforced across all four canonical surfaces: `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`, `~/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md`, this convention, and `~/ai/workflows/code-quality.md`.\n\n### Bootstrap exception\n\nA Work Unit whose primary deliverable is itself a change to the code-quality metric, convention, verifier, or auditor surface may, narrowly, advance past a pipeline-callable code-quality gate that scores `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` on the intrinsic lockstep elements that the WU's own change is rewriting. The exception applies only when ALL of the following conditions are met:\n\n1. The WU's primary deliverable fixes or extends the code-quality metric or rule currently scoring the WU non-LOW.\n2. The non-LOW finding is on intrinsic lockstep elements required by the metric / convention / verifier / auditor change itself, not on collateral product code.\n3. The post-merge codebase satisfies the new rule under the new metric — i.e. once the WU lands, the same audit on the new tree returns LOW.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/nes/ai/conventions/code-quality.md","text":"\n- LOW: `agents/provider-quota-filter.md` is declared with `role: intrinsic-surface`, `Domain: quota_state`, and `Owns:` containing `provider_quotas`, `provider_quota_windows`, `exhausted_at`, `resets_at`, `filtered_indices`, and `clear_exhausted`. The component covers 1 domain and all external references are subordinate to the declared quota-state symbols and operations.\n- HIGH: `agents/provider-quota-filter.md` declares `role: intrinsic-surface` for `Domain: quota_state` but also reads routing-log internals or writes billing records outside `Owns:`. The intrinsic-surface declaration does not apply as LOW because non-subordinate references are reached.\n\n\n## Disposition policy\n\nOnly LOW passes pipeline-callable code-quality gates. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` block advance, trigger remediation/revise, and require a rerun from current evidence. Neither severity is ever a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user choice, or a \"stable\" allow-advance state for code-quality / risk-gate / prototype-risk verdicts.\n\nFor current, well-formed semantic `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` findings, `~/ai/conventions/decomposition-strategies.md` selects remediation, MOVE, split, or decomposition handling. Neither severity becomes a residual or user-disposition state through that selector.\n\nThis rule is the ACR-156 parent regression disposition and is enforced across all four canonical surfaces: `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`, `~/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md`, this convention, and `~/ai/workflows/code-quality.md`.\n\n### Bootstrap exception\n\nA Work Unit whose primary deliverable is itself a change to the code-quality metric, convention, verifier, or auditor surface may, narrowly, advance past a pipeline-callable code-quality gate that scores `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` on the intrinsic lockstep elements that the WU's own change is rewriting. The exception applies only when ALL of the following conditions are met:\n\n1. The WU's primary deliverable fixes or extends the code-quality metric or rule currently scoring the WU non-LOW.\n2. The non-LOW finding is on intrinsic lockstep elements required by the metric / convention / verifier / auditor change itself, not on collateral product code.\n3. The post-merge codebase satisfies the new rule under the new metric — i.e. once the WU lands, the same audit on the new tree returns LOW.\n4. The exception is declared in Phase 3 of `~/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md` and ratified in the Phase 4 join manifest as a `bootstrap-exception` row marked `RATIFIED`, with a corresponding `### <ticket-id> — Bootstrap exception ratification` entry in `${worktree_path}/DECISIONS.md` citing this subsection.\n\nThis is the ONLY local carve-out from the LOW-only rule above. Ordinary residual acceptance, `NEEDS_INPUT` user choice, stable-allow-advance, or any manager-flavor / root / DECISIONS-only acceptance of a non-LOW pipeline-callable code-quality verdict remains forbidden under `~/ai/conventions/workflow-execution-violations.md` § `Named anti-pattern: Non-LOW gate residual acceptance`. Missing the Phase 3 declaration, missing the DECISIONS ratification entry, missing the citation to this subsection, or missing the Phase 4 join-manifest `bootstrap-exception` row marked `RATIFIED` falls through to the LOW-only blocking rule above.\n\n### Oscillation signals WU-too-large\n\nAfter two consecutive rounds that do not converge to `LOW`, the Work Unit is too large for the current grain and must decompose instead of attempting a third remediation pass. Decompose is autonomous, not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated; the caller records the oscillation evidence and opens the smaller WU path required by the owning workflow.\n\nAfter this two-round non-convergence trigger fires, `~/ai/conventions/decomposition-strategies.md` names the smaller-WU shape and any follow-up-ticket handoff evidence. It does not make decompose `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated.\n\n## Numerical thresholds\n\nThese are starting calibration points for downstream auditors A4 / A5 / A6 (NES-140 / NES-141 / NES-148). Those auditors may refine measurement details, but they should treat these thresholds as the initial shared baseline.\n\n| Metric | LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Nesting depth` | 0-1 | n/a | >= 2 |\n| `Function categories per function` | 1 | n/a | >= 2 |\n| `Cohesion by classifications touched` | actual classifications are a subset of the declared role set (file-local, path default, or component-level declared roles in a Phase 6a contract); for components and files without any declared roles, exactly 1 classification | n/a | actual classifications exceed the declared role set or include classifications outside the declared role set; for components and files without any declared roles, 2 or more classifications |\n| `Coupling by distinct external symbols/modules referenced` | 0-2 | 3-5 | >= 6 |\n\nThe first two rows are hard code-shape thresholds in this convention. The cohesion and coupling rows are review calibration: they identify where a function is likely doing too many kinds of work or depending on too many external surfaces.\n\n## Failure modes\n\n- `multi-classifier function`\n- `deep-nest violator`\n- `inline-function carrier`\n- `duplicate-responsibility carrier`\n- `uncontrolled-source coupler`\n\n## What this convention does not enforce\n\nThis convention is policy, not enforcement. Auditors and future WUs enforce it. No linter, CI gate, pre-commit hook, workflow gate, language-specific style configuration, or runtime checker is established by this file.\n\nIt also does not replace project-local architecture rules. A project may have stricter framework, package, API, or naming rules; those rules layer on top of this convention when they do not conflict with it.\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- `~/ai/conventions/decomposition-strategies.md` - selector for remediation, MOVE, split, or decomposition after existing ownership, LOW-only, bootstrap, and oscillation signals fire.\n- `~/ai/workflows/code-quality.md` - composite dispatch workflow for existing A1 auditors; this is an operational pointer only and does not change this convention's rule text, failure modes, numerical thresholds, or policy-only status.\n- `~/ai/workflows/code-quality.md` § `Stop Conditions And Escalation` - separates standalone advisory use from pipeline-callable blocking use; pipeline-callable blocking enforces this disposition policy, including LOW-only passage and autonomous decomposition after oscillation.\n- `~/ai/agents/function-classification-auditor.md` - A5 / NES-141 enforcement surface for the `Function categories per function` threshold and `multi-classifier function` findings; this is a forward pointer only and does not change this convention's categories, rule, failure modes, thresholds, or policy-only status.\n- `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Scoring axes - duplicate-system count, change-path entropy, and risk scoring mechanics.\n- `~/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md` § Step 2.5.4 and § Phase 3 - duplicate-systems inventory plus cascade/consolidate/accept handling.\n- `~/ai/conventions/agent-questions-and-session-graph.md` § Pull-vs-Push Policy - terminology disambiguation only.\n- `~/ai/agents/push-pull-auditor.md` - A4 / NES-140 enforcement surface for the `Push-vs-pull system coupling` rule and `uncontrolled-source coupler` findings; this is a forward pointer only and does not change this convention's rule text, failure modes, numerical thresholds, or policy-only status.\n- `~/ai/conventions/workflow-execution-violations.md` § `Named anti-pattern: Non-LOW gate residual acceptance` - the forbidden baseline this convention's `### Bootstrap exception` is narrowly carved from; manager / root / DECISIONS-only acceptance of a non-LOW pipeline-callable code-quality verdict remains a blocking gate/termination violation outside the four-condition path declared and ratified per § `Bootstrap exception`.","lineStart":261,"lineEnd":328,"totalLines":328,"truncated":false}},"title":"../../../../ai/conventions/code-quality.md","time":{"start":1780803012749,"end":1780803012820}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_081ae533f4033423016a24e5c4a97481978ffade767ba1fe14"}},"id":"prt_ea0218865001jP91wkm9q65vv3","sessionID":"ses_15fdf386fffe9fkXvMGWNEHLbs","messageID":"msg_ea02170eb0010We2gZSRNkAbkT"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780803012846,"sessionID":"ses_15fdf386fffe9fkXvMGWNEHLbs","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_XsvUkBROI0iNoOJP97x2Ndfx","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/ai/agents/coupling-auditor.md","offset":241,"limit":60},"output":"<path>/home/nes/ai/agents/coupling-auditor.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n241: \n242: ## Escalation\n243: \n244: - If cross-language dependency edges are necessary and not present, request or consume `code-tracer.md` evidence; do not replace this verdict with a trace-only report.\n245: - If A1's coupling metric row is missing, renamed, or contradicted, return `BLOCKED:A1-metric-source` with the specific missing or conflicting row.\n246: - If the caller asks for sibling enforcement, return out of scope and name the appropriate sibling auditor/WU boundary.\n247: - If the evidence invalidates a load-bearing proposal assumption, report the invalidation and recommend returning to research before scoring further.\n\n(End of file - total 247 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\n## Escalation\n\n- If cross-language dependency edges are necessary and not present, request or consume `code-tracer.md` evidence; do not replace this verdict with a trace-only report.\n- If A1's coupling metric row is missing, renamed, or contradicted, return `BLOCKED:A1-metric-source` with the specific missing or conflicting row.\n- If the caller asks for sibling enforcement, return out of scope and name the appropriate sibling auditor/WU boundary.\n- If the evidence invalidates a load-bearing proposal assumption, report the invalidation and recommend returning to research before scoring further.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/nes/ai/agents/coupling-auditor.md","text":"\n## Escalation\n\n- If cross-language dependency edges are necessary and not present, request or consume `code-tracer.md` evidence; do not replace this verdict with a trace-only report.\n- If A1's coupling metric row is missing, renamed, or contradicted, return `BLOCKED:A1-metric-source` with the specific missing or conflicting row.\n- If the caller asks for sibling enforcement, return out of scope and name the appropriate sibling auditor/WU boundary.\n- If the evidence invalidates a load-bearing proposal assumption, report the invalidation and recommend returning to research before scoring further.","lineStart":241,"lineEnd":247,"totalLines":247,"truncated":false}},"title":"../../../../ai/agents/coupling-auditor.md","time":{"start":1780803012783,"end":1780803012825}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_081ae533f4033423016a24e5c4a99081978a8bbf7d4de708a8"}},"id":"prt_ea02188a7001v3RqRLWonBxOe7","sessionID":"ses_15fdf386fffe9fkXvMGWNEHLbs","messageID":"msg_ea02170eb0010We2gZSRNkAbkT"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780803012848,"sessionID":"ses_15fdf386fffe9fkXvMGWNEHLbs","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_F3gmO9IWkMUAwe0Xk3KBu3sO","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","offset":500,"limit":160},"output":"<path>/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n500: - Rule: the post-prototype derivation record is `LevelComponentSet`; in addition to the derivation-record fields above, it requires `layer_level_id`, `component_pair_refs[]`, `integration_test_refs`, and `coverage_summary`. `component_pair_refs[]` is the integration-test-targeted subset of pairs derivable from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims`. `coverage_summary` must distinguish \"no interacting pairs at this layer\" from an interacting pair with missing test refs or missing integration coverage.\n501: - Rule: `LevelComponentSet` trace text may summarize the pairing source as component_pair_refs[]derived from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims` when linking the accepted component inventory to required integration coverage.\n502: - Rule: Coupling-driven component-set evolution. After Step 6c has passing level evidence and a candidate or accepted current-layer component inventory exists, coupling examination may change that inventory in either direction: it may merge component elements together when the evidence shows they are one component, or it may introduce an abstraction component when the evidence shows shared dependencies should be decoupled through an intermediate component. Coupling-driven changes stay at the current `level_id`; nested sub-components, grandchild components, and multi-layer hierarchy still route through recursive child entry, not through this rule.\n503: - Rule: `CouplingDecision` records component-set evolution from coupling evidence. It is recorded adjacent to the current `LevelComponentSet` evidence, or adjacent to the candidate component inventory when no `LevelComponentSet` has been accepted yet. Required fields are `level_id`, `source_level_component_set_ref`, `candidate_component_inventory_ref` when no accepted `LevelComponentSet` exists, `coupling_audit_report_refs`, `cohesion_audit_report_refs` when the decision depends on per-component organization evidence, `verdict`, `changed_component_refs`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, `new_abstraction_component_refs`, `integration_test_refs`, `rationale_evidence_refs`, and `defer_to_prototype_risk`.\n504: - Rule: `CouplingDecision.verdict` is exactly one of `merge_components`, `introduce_abstraction_component`, or `no_change`. `merge_components` means the current-level component inventory collapses the affected component refs into one component. `introduce_abstraction_component` means the current-level component inventory adds a new abstraction component to decouple shared dependencies. `no_change` means coupling/cohesion evidence was considered and the current component inventory remains unchanged. A `no_change` verdict does not bypass `HaltRecord` or no-split evidence when existing derivation or halt rules require them.\n505: - Rule: Evidence-bearing halt rule: a level halts only when candidate splits earn granularity under none of the `halt_basis` options: `clarify contracts`, `reduce accidental coupling`, `expose a design challenge`, `improve evidence`, `lower meaningful risk`. The `halt_basis` field records option-level evidence showing why each listed option was unsatisfied. The canonical halt record path is `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-halt-record.md`. The implementation-pipeline orchestrator checks this rule procedurally per `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § `#### Phase 6 halt-state transition gate`.\n506: - Rule: `HaltRecord` required fields are `halt_basis`, `component_candidates_considered`, `evidence_refs`, and `residual_risk_refs`. `component_candidates_considered` is required and lists considered or rejected split candidates so the halt is replayable.\n507: - Rule: an interacting component pair listed in `component_pair_refs[]` without corresponding `integration_test_refs` is not non-applicability and must not be silently skipped; it halts with violation code `integration_test_missing` and requires replayable missing-pair evidence naming which pair and what expected coverage was missing.\n508: - Rule: Halt verdict-resolution rule: halt-by-omission must not override a split or revise verdict. An accepted halt that conflicts with an overlay verdict cites the conflicting overlay verdict evidence and the evidence justifying halt; unresolved accepted conflicts are recorded as Decision Recording entries named `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual`.\n509: - Rule: Process-tree review: after Step 6c completes and before Phase 7, dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)`. The operator-owned expected-process manifest and process-tree audit #2 must prove separate Step 6b and Step 6c invocations, timing order, Step 6b output index presence, Step 6b output paths, ACR-247 side-channel consumption evidence carried by the expected-process manifest for those outputs, test-contract alignment, prototype risk review, per-component code-quality, the derivation record or no-split / rejection subsection when a derivation trigger fires, `HaltRecord` or halt/no-split evidence when halt logic fires, procedural handoff evidence for Step 6c-discovered obligations, and `CouplingDecision` evidence with rerun/current `integration_test_refs` when coupling examination changes or confirms the current component inventory. Missing, malformed, mismatched, stale, late, wrong-scope side-file evidence, or a currentness mismatch under `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema` and § `Row-level re-verification` is blocking. A returned `BLOCKED`, `NEEDS_INPUT`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `STALE_REFUSAL`, or process-tree blocking row prevents Phase 7; the affected subtree is rerun or repaired before downstream consumption.\n510: - Rule: Phase 6 -> Phase 7 `PrototypeSwapRecord` boundary: this rule sits after Step 6c's process-tree review and before Phase 7; Phase 6 still has exactly three sub-steps, Step 6a, Step 6b, and Step 6c, so this is not a Step 6d. Before Phase 7 can advance or any optional PR-mode review can consume the diff, every WU or level scope with a level prototype must produce a `PrototypeSwapRecord` at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; a WU or level scope without a level prototype must place an explicit non-applicability statement at that same path, because a missing artifact is not equivalent to non-applicability. The record must contain `component_refs`, `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, `level_behavior_test_results`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, and `audit_overlay_refs`, with `level_id` and `prototype_ref` additional to the six ticket fields, plus a terminal-state field whose allowed values are `consumed`, `non-applicable`, and `superseded`. Each `audit_overlay_refs` entry must identify the artifact path and the verdict or currentness evidence proving the artifact applies to the swapped component inventory. The canonical implementation-pipeline workflow forbids the implementation-pipeline orchestrator from advancing past the Phase 7 readiness checks on a diff that has no `PrototypeSwapRecord` or explicit non-applicability statement at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; Phase 7 dispatch cannot advance and the optional review operator cannot consume that diff. This rule authorizes no new operators and no new workflow file, and it does not define overlay scheduling, which belongs to sibling NES-270. This prohibition is implemented by the `Pre-dispatch swap-record gate` in `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § Phase 7, which the orchestrator agent executes procedurally. Parallel prototype code cannot remain as shadow implementation unless the proposal at the level scope has named retained ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n511: - Boundary pointer: same-layer component-pair integration-test evidence is upstream evidence consumed before swap readiness and is distinct from `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, and `level_behavior_test_results`.\n512: \n513: ## Phase 7 - Optional PR-Mode Review Delegation\n514: \n515: The implementation-pipeline orchestrator runs readiness checks, then calls `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py` directly for optional PR-mode CodeRabbit review. The script owns @mention triggering, trigger-ack polling, GitHub review-state interpretation, review/comment normalization, and per-comment body persistence. The repository-level `coderabbit` label is only an installation marker; applying it to a PR suppresses CodeRabbit and is not a trigger.\n516: \n517: - Readiness checks: inherited prototype proof tests, integration-test evidence for same-layer interacting component pairs, and `PrototypeSwapRecord` / explicit non-applicability.\n518: - Enablement primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py is-enabled ${repo}`. Exit `1` is a clean skip. Other nonzero exits block Phase 7.\n519: - Trigger mode criterion: use `incremental` for normal PR-review flow and for re-runs after child fix commits. Use `full` only for code-audit or mass-cleanup workflows whose declared review target is whole files rather than the latest diff, such as file-by-file risk-assessment cleanup passes.\n520: - Loop primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py review-loop ${repo} ${pr_num} --worktree-path ${worktree_path} --mode ${coderabbit_trigger_mode}`.\n521: - The driver owns the full loop: trigger or auto-skip an already-pending trigger, wait for ack, enforce the 300-second minimum cadence between loop-owned polls, dispatch one fixer invocation per actionable in-diff comment, preserve each structured outcome, push fixed commits, post reply files, trigger incremental follow-up reviews, and continue until terminal.\n522: - `terminal_reason=approved` is CodeRabbit success.\n523: - `terminal_reason=no_value_provided` is accepted convergence when every actionable in-diff comment in the latest iteration was assessed `review_provided_value: false`.\n524: - `needs_caller_decision=true` surfaces valuable `rejected` or `deferred` per-comment outcomes to the root caller without re-triggering.\n525: - The orchestrator must not compose its own trigger/poll/dispatch/retrigger loop, must not use inline `gh pr view ... statusCheckRollup` polling, must not inline CodeRabbit comment bodies, and must not synthesize an empty review pass.\n526: - If the pipeline creates or reuses a PR, it writes `${scratch_dir}/pr-url.txt`; Phase 9 reuses that URL instead of creating a duplicate PR.\n527: \n528: ## Phase 8 - Post-Review Gates\n529: \n530: - These are the PR-opening gates on the actual diff.\n531: - Policy: dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-8)` after Phase 7 readiness and optional review delegation, before draft PR movement.\n532: - The Phase 8 gate-set inventory is test-audit review, multi-concern PR review, justification PR review, commit hygiene, Phase 8 actual-diff code-quality, proof-risk and validation-integrity runtime-claim transport where applicable, supported-surface inventory-resolution while ACR-286 remains open, currentness rechecks against prior joins, `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-8-join-manifest.json`, and process-tree audit #3.\n533: - Rule: the actual PR diff is the review target. Proposal-shaped evidence cannot satisfy actual-diff rows.\n534: - Rule: `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-8)` writes or verifies the Phase 8 join manifest at `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-8-join-manifest.json`, projects expected-process evidence, and requires process-tree audit #3 evidence. Rows carry at least `canonical_output_path`, `size`, `mtime`, `sha256`, `verdict_line`, and `verified_at`, plus the operator schema's caller mode, raw/normalized verdict, applicability, currentness, inventory-resolution, and blocking-status fields. Currentness rechecks against prior joins follow `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema`, § `Row-level re-verification`, and § `Full re-dispatch`.\n535: - Rule: the Phase 8 code-quality rows must carry actual-diff runtime-claim context under the Phase 3 `## Proof plan` rule. Validation-integrity and proof-risk remain required where that context makes them applicable.\n536: - Rule: if the multi-concern row says the diff should be split, split it before opening PRs.\n537: - Rule: preserve ACR-280 Phase 8 code-quality strategy selection. For current semantic Phase 8 code-quality `MEDIUM` or `HIGH`, choose MOVE-and-import and record `STRATEGY_PHASE8_CODE_QUALITY_MOVE_AND_IMPORT`, in-place file-decomposition and record `STRATEGY_PHASE8_CODE_QUALITY_FILE_DECOMPOSITION`, in-WU remediation/helper extraction and record `STRATEGY_PHASE8_CODE_QUALITY_INWU`, or follow-up-ticket decomposition and record `STRATEGY_PHASE8_CODE_QUALITY_FOLLOWUP`. Follow-up tickets require current-WU narrowing and rerun or explicit `DECOMPOSED`; they never allow advance by themselves.\n538: - Rule: only a returned `PASS` from `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-8)` permits closure judge dispatch, draft PR creation, ticket comment work, or PR movement. `BLOCKED`, `NEEDS_INPUT`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `STALE_REFUSAL`, stale rows, missing output paths, malformed manifests, or process-tree blocking findings prevent draft PR creation until the affected subtree is rerun or repaired.\n539: - Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the root handles it through `~/ai/conventions/agent-questions-and-session-graph.md`. Do not advance this phase, write code, post review output, or open a PR from work that depends on the unanswered question.\n540: - Phase 8 is the review and capture layer that precedes draft PR creation; it is not followed by a human branch-local review fallback before Phase 9.\n541: - Phase 8 capture documentation: after `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-8)` returns `PASS` with process-tree audit #3 evidence cleared, the orchestrator dispatches the `gpt-xhigh` closure judge and records `actual_story_points` and `actual_capture_method` as an orchestrator-owned closure artifact before Phase 9. The enum is `closer-best-effort | wall-time-derived | unmeasured`, the point set is `1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 40, 100`, and wall-time-derived is reserved as enum-only with no requirements for timers, durations, start/stop timestamps, or trace-derived time calculations.\n542: - Rule: ACR-125 capture must not dispatch `task=update-estimate` for actuals, must not write actuals to Linear `estimate`, and must not write actuals to Jira `customfield_10016`; live ticket estimate fields continue to hold the refined estimate.\n543: - Final verification-only behavior: Final does not recalculate the actual; it verifies the audit-history calibration block and `estimate_comparison_comment_ref`, then references the comparison in the close-comment.\n544: - Final halt-on-missing: on a missing/invalid/inconsistent calibration block or invalid `estimate_comparison_comment_ref`, Final halts and must not post the close-comment. Final verification accepts `<id | url | none>`, rejects `path`, and treats none valid only when `ticket_system: jira`. On success, Final close-comment includes inherited/refined/actual values inline on both backends.\n545: \n546: ```worked example\n547: Phase 8 worked example\n548: \n549: inherited_story_point_estimate: 3\n550: refined_story_point_estimate: 5\n551: actual_story_points: 8\n552: actual_capture_method: closer-best-effort\n553: estimate_delta_narrative: inherited=3; refined=5; actual=8; delta_refined_to_actual=3; over_2x_inherited=true\n554: \n555: sample comment body\n556: \n557: Estimate calibration\n558: \n559: - inherited_story_point_estimate: 3\n560: - refined_story_point_estimate: 5\n561: - actual_story_points: 8\n562: - actual_capture_method: closer-best-effort\n563: - actual_estimate_rationale: closer-best-effort: closure scope landed broader than the refined estimate assumed.\n564: - estimate_delta_narrative: inherited=3; refined=5; actual=8; delta_refined_to_actual=3; over_2x_inherited=true\n565: - audit_history: ${planning_dir}/audit-history.md\n566: ```\n567: \n568: ## Phase 9 - Draft PR\n569: \n570: - Routine automation step after all prior gates pass.\n571: - Rule: if Phase 7 wrote `${scratch_dir}/pr-url.txt`, Phase 9 verifies and reuses that PR instead of running `gh pr create` again.\n572: - Rule: open one draft PR per concern identified by multi-concern review.\n573: - Rule: respect dependency order for stacked work.\n574: - Rule: follow `~/ai/conventions/git.md`.\n575: - Rule: opening a **draft** PR is not Tier-3 by default.\n576: - Rule: **the title and body MUST be authored by `~/ai/agents/pr-writer.md`** — never hand-written by the orchestrator and never inlined into the `gh pr create` invocation. The writer enforces audience-and-content rules (no internal jargon, no commit-history sections, no closed-PR or local-planning-artifact references) that hand-written or templated bodies routinely violate. Pass the relevant phase artifacts (`problem-map.md`, `proposal.md`, `contract.md`, RCA evidence) as `context_files` so the writer has intent grounding without citing them in the body. For stacked PRs, supply `stack_parent_pr=<num>`; for any reference to merged work, supply `merged_refs=<list>`; when the selected ticket backend is Linear and the Linear key is known, supply `linear_issue_keys=<KEY>` so `pr-writer` emits the close-keyword footer itself.\n577: - Rule: RCA-track. The draft PR description includes before/after evidence for every Phase 0 red signal, whether a newly authored reproduction test or an existing failing test accepted as the reproduction signal: the red-run command and output or accepted failure evidence, paired with the matching post-fix green-run command and output recorded in Step 6c. Pass the evidence path(s) to `pr-writer` via `context_files` so they're folded into the body in audience-safe form.\n578: \n579: ## Phase 10 - Promote To Ready-For-Review\n580: \n581: - Automated by the orchestrator once Phase 9 succeeds and the Phase 8 process-tree audit has cleared.\n582: - Rule: the orchestrator runs `gh pr ready <pr_number>` and records the promotion in `${scratch_dir}/pr-promoted.txt`. No human gate.\n583: \n584: ## Gate Ownership\n585: \n586: See `~/ai/conventions/gate-ownership.md` for the phase-by-phase table.\n587: \n588: Rule of thumb:\n589: - the implementation-pipeline human gates are (1) the Phase 2.5 problem-map review and (2) legitimate NEEDS_INPUT new-value-question surfacing per `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`\n590: - every other phase belongs to a model gate or to automation\n591: - the proposal is reviewed by the risk gate, not by an extra human approval pass\n592: - Phase 9 (draft PR) and Phase 10 (promotion) are both automated by the orchestrator\n593: \n594: ## Tiered Approval\n595: \n596: Anything with user-visible side effects follows `~/ai/workflows/tiered-approval.md`.\n597: \n598: Practical default:\n599: - drafting code is not Tier-3\n600: - editing files is not Tier-3\n601: - running tests is not Tier-3\n602: - opening **draft** PRs is not Tier-3\n603: - public promotion and other outward-facing actions follow the tiered-approval workflow\n604: \n605: ## Pause For Refactor\n606: \n607: This section owns the pause state model for existing-code refactor work discovered during an implementation WU. For the strategy boundary, cite `conventions/feature-development-workflow.md` `## Refactoring out of scope`; do not restate that rule elsewhere in this workflow.\n608: \n609: - trigger: an auditor, reviewer, or implementation step identifies existing-code structural refactor work that is outside touched-file/component ownership, or inside touched ownership but too large for the current WU, and material to continuing safely.\n610: - pause evidence: record the current ticket id, branch, head SHA, worktree path, dirty status, failing or residual report paths, and the reason the refactor is outside touched ownership or requires decomposition.\n611: - ticket/backend action: use the selected ticket backend to comment the pause evidence on the current ticket and apply the available pause/blocking status or label; if no status exists, use a clear blocking comment plus link relation.\n612: - refactor-ticket filing: file a linked refactor-ticket with the bounded structural target, preserved-behavior requirement, evidence paths, and relationship to the paused WU.\n613: - ACR-179 routing: run the refactor-ticket through the ACR-179 refactoring route, using `workflows/refactoring.md` and `agents/refactoring-orchestrator.md`.\n614: - resume: resume the paused WU only after the refactor-ticket is closed, superseded, or explicitly dispositioned by the active manager flavor with recorded evidence.\n615: - touched-file/component gate failures: this pause path does not alter touched-file/component ownership; current-WU `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` code-quality findings still revise, remediate, or decompose under the existing LOW-only rules.\n616: \n617: ## Decision Recording\n618: \n619: <!-- INTENTIONAL: \"deferred\" here is a decision-recording state category, not an unwired workflow stub. -->\n620: If a phase is skipped, narrowed, deferred, terminated for non-positive value, sent back to research on invalidated assumptions, or accepted with a named unverifiable residual risk that does not collapse the approved net-value case under the supported-surface termination rule:\n621: - record it in `${worktree_path}/DECISIONS.md` or an explicitly supplied project equivalent\n622: - record who made the decision\n623: - record why the change was accepted\n624: - record what risk is being accepted\n625: - record what evidence caused the termination or research re-entry\n626: \n627: For Phase 6 firstness, do not create a new residual class. If a named test-verification risk remains unverified, record it in `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`. If only historical or process-provenance evidence is unverifiable and the approved net-value case still holds, record the decision only as `accepted with a named unverifiable residual risk` in `${worktree_path}/DECISIONS.md` or an explicitly supplied project equivalent.\n628: \n629: Accepted halt residuals and accepted `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual` entries follow this same Decision Recording rule when they do not collapse the approved net-value case.\n630: \n631: Decision Recording, generic residual recording, and Phase 7 / Phase 8 residual / value-zero review language do NOT authorize residual acceptance of Phase 4 code-quality, Phase 6 prototype-risk, or per-component code-quality `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` verdicts. Those gate verdicts revise, remediate, or decompose per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Disposition policy` and § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`; they are never recorded as residuals. Generic residual recording remains valid for non-gate residual classes only (bounded test-verification residuals in `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, process-provenance uncertainty per the Phase 6 firstness paragraph above, accepted halt residuals, accepted `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual` entries, and Phase 8 value-zero termination evidence).\n632: \n633: Without that record, the phase was not skipped correctly.\n634: \n635: ## Orchestrator\n636: \n637: This pipeline is dispatched by the `implementation-pipeline-orchestrator` (`gpt-xhigh`) operator at `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`. The orchestrator is the only delegated actor that walks a Work Unit through every phase, dispatches each leaf operator via the `agents` CLI, runs the three required process-tree audits, and enforces the violation-escalation policy autonomously (including Tier-1 main-branch rewinds when a violation is detected). Inlining the orchestrator role into the root conversation is itself a workflow violation because it removes the orchestration decisions from `agents trace --json` and prevents process-tree-auditor from auditing the workflow as a whole.\n638: \n639: ## Adjacent References\n640: \n641: - `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`\n642: - `~/ai/models/roles.md`\n643: - `~/ai/workflows/agents-cli.md`\n644: - `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`\n645: - `~/ai/conventions/git.md`\n646: - `~/ai/agents/coderabbit-operator.md`\n647: - `~/ai/workflows/pr-review.md`\n648: - `~/ai/workflows/tiered-approval.md`\n649: - `~/ai/conventions/no-backwards-compatibility.md`\n650: - `~/ai/conventions/no-deferred-stubs.md`\n651: - `~/ai/conventions/gate-ownership.md`\n652: - `~/ai/conventions/audit-history.md`\n\n(End of file - total 652 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- Rule: the post-prototype derivation record is `LevelComponentSet`; in addition to the derivation-record fields above, it requires `layer_level_id`, `component_pair_refs[]`, `integration_test_refs`, and `coverage_summary`. `component_pair_refs[]` is the integration-test-targeted subset of pairs derivable from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims`. `coverage_summary` must distinguish \"no interacting pairs at this layer\" from an interacting pair with missing test refs or missing integration coverage.\n- Rule: `LevelComponentSet` trace text may summarize the pairing source as component_pair_refs[]derived from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims` when linking the accepted component inventory to required integration coverage.\n- Rule: Coupling-driven component-set evolution. After Step 6c has passing level evidence and a candidate or accepted current-layer component inventory exists, coupling examination may change that inventory in either direction: it may merge component elements together when the evidence shows they are one component, or it may introduce an abstraction component when the evidence shows shared dependencies should be decoupled through an intermediate component. Coupling-driven changes stay at the current `level_id`; nested sub-components, grandchild components, and multi-layer hierarchy still route through recursive child entry, not through this rule.\n- Rule: `CouplingDecision` records component-set evolution from coupling evidence. It is recorded adjacent to the current `LevelComponentSet` evidence, or adjacent to the candidate component inventory when no `LevelComponentSet` has been accepted yet. Required fields are `level_id`, `source_level_component_set_ref`, `candidate_component_inventory_ref` when no accepted `LevelComponentSet` exists, `coupling_audit_report_refs`, `cohesion_audit_report_refs` when the decision depends on per-component organization evidence, `verdict`, `changed_component_refs`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, `new_abstraction_component_refs`, `integration_test_refs`, `rationale_evidence_refs`, and `defer_to_prototype_risk`.\n- Rule: `CouplingDecision.verdict` is exactly one of `merge_components`, `introduce_abstraction_component`, or `no_change`. `merge_components` means the current-level component inventory collapses the affected component refs into one component. `introduce_abstraction_component` means the current-level component inventory adds a new abstraction component to decouple shared dependencies. `no_change` means coupling/cohesion evidence was considered and the current component inventory remains unchanged. A `no_change` verdict does not bypass `HaltRecord` or no-split evidence when existing derivation or halt rules require them.\n- Rule: Evidence-bearing halt rule: a level halts only when candidate splits earn granularity under none of the `halt_basis` options: `clarify contracts`, `reduce accidental coupling`, `expose a design challenge`, `improve evidence`, `lower meaningful risk`. The `halt_basis` field records option-level evidence showing why each listed option was unsatisfied. The canonical halt record path is `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-halt-record.md`. The implementation-pipeline orchestrator checks this rule procedurally per `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § `#### Phase 6 halt-state transition gate`.\n- Rule: `HaltRecord` required fields are `halt_basis`, `component_candidates_considered`, `evidence_refs`, and `residual_risk_refs`. `component_candidates_considered` is required and lists considered or rejected split candidates so the halt is replayable.\n- Rule: an interacting component pair listed in `component_pair_refs[]` without corresponding `integration_test_refs` is not non-applicability and must not be silently skipped; it halts with violation code `integration_test_missing` and requires replayable missing-pair evidence naming which pair and what expected coverage was missing.\n- Rule: Halt verdict-resolution rule: halt-by-omission must not override a split or revise verdict. An accepted halt that conflicts with an overlay verdict cites the conflicting overlay verdict evidence and the evidence justifying halt; unresolved accepted conflicts are recorded as Decision Recording entries named `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual`.\n- Rule: Process-tree review: after Step 6c completes and before Phase 7, dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)`. The operator-owned expected-process manifest and process-tree audit #2 must prove separate Step 6b and Step 6c invocations, timing order, Step 6b output index presence, Step 6b output paths, ACR-247 side-channel consumption evidence carried by the expected-process manifest for those outputs, test-contract alignment, prototype risk review, per-component code-quality, the derivation record or no-split / rejection subsection when a derivation trigger fires, `HaltRecord` or halt/no-split evidence when halt logic fires, procedural handoff evidence for Step 6c-discovered obligations, and `CouplingDecision` evidence with rerun/current `integration_test_refs` when coupling examination changes or confirms the current component inventory. Missing, malformed, mismatched, stale, late, wrong-scope side-file evidence, or a currentness mismatch under `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema` and § `Row-level re-verification` is blocking. A returned `BLOCKED`, `NEEDS_INPUT`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `STALE_REFUSAL`, or process-tree blocking row prevents Phase 7; the affected subtree is rerun or repaired before downstream consumption.\n- Rule: Phase 6 -> Phase 7 `PrototypeSwapRecord` boundary: this rule sits after Step 6c's process-tree review and before Phase 7; Phase 6 still has exactly three sub-steps, Step 6a, Step 6b, and Step 6c, so this is not a Step 6d. Before Phase 7 can advance or any optional PR-mode review can consume the diff, every WU or level scope with a level prototype must produce a `PrototypeSwapRecord` at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; a WU or level scope without a level prototype must place an explicit non-applicability statement at that same path, because a missing artifact is not equivalent to non-applicability. The record must contain `component_refs`, `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, `level_behavior_test_results`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, and `audit_overlay_refs`, with `level_id` and `prototype_ref` additional to the six ticket fields, plus a terminal-state field whose allowed values are `consumed`, `non-applicable`, and `superseded`. Each `audit_overlay_refs` entry must identify the artifact path and the verdict or currentness evidence proving the artifact applies to the swapped component inventory. The canonical implementation-pipeline workflow forbids the implementation-pipeline orchestrator from advancing past the Phase 7 readiness checks on a diff that has no `PrototypeSwapRecord` or explicit non-applicability statement at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; Phase 7 dispatch cannot advance and the optional review operator cannot consume that diff. This rule authorizes no new operators and no new workflow file, and it does not define overlay scheduling, which belongs to sibling NES-270. This prohibition is implemented by the `Pre-dispatch swap-record gate` in `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § Phase 7, which the orchestrator agent executes procedurally. Parallel prototype code cannot remain as shadow implementation unless the proposal at the level scope has named retained ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n- Boundary pointer: same-layer component-pair integration-test evidence is upstream evidence consumed before swap readiness and is distinct from `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, and `level_behavior_test_results`.\n\n## Phase 7 - Optional PR-Mode Review Delegation\n\nThe implementation-pipeline orchestrator runs readiness checks, then calls `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py` directly for optional PR-mode CodeRabbit review. The script owns @mention triggering, trigger-ack polling, GitHub review-state interpretation, review/comment normalization, and per-comment body persistence. The repository-level `coderabbit` label is only an installation marker; applying it to a PR suppresses CodeRabbit and is not a trigger.\n\n- Readiness checks: inherited prototype proof tests, integration-test evidence for same-layer interacting component pairs, and `PrototypeSwapRecord` / explicit non-applicability.\n- Enablement primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py is-enabled ${repo}`. Exit `1` is a clean skip. Other nonzero exits block Phase 7.\n- Trigger mode criterion: use `incremental` for normal PR-review flow and for re-runs after child fix commits. Use `full` only for code-audit or mass-cleanup workflows whose declared review target is whole files rather than the latest diff, such as file-by-file risk-assessment cleanup passes.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","text":"- Rule: the post-prototype derivation record is `LevelComponentSet`; in addition to the derivation-record fields above, it requires `layer_level_id`, `component_pair_refs[]`, `integration_test_refs`, and `coverage_summary`. `component_pair_refs[]` is the integration-test-targeted subset of pairs derivable from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims`. `coverage_summary` must distinguish \"no interacting pairs at this layer\" from an interacting pair with missing test refs or missing integration coverage.\n- Rule: `LevelComponentSet` trace text may summarize the pairing source as component_pair_refs[]derived from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims` when linking the accepted component inventory to required integration coverage.\n- Rule: Coupling-driven component-set evolution. After Step 6c has passing level evidence and a candidate or accepted current-layer component inventory exists, coupling examination may change that inventory in either direction: it may merge component elements together when the evidence shows they are one component, or it may introduce an abstraction component when the evidence shows shared dependencies should be decoupled through an intermediate component. Coupling-driven changes stay at the current `level_id`; nested sub-components, grandchild components, and multi-layer hierarchy still route through recursive child entry, not through this rule.\n- Rule: `CouplingDecision` records component-set evolution from coupling evidence. It is recorded adjacent to the current `LevelComponentSet` evidence, or adjacent to the candidate component inventory when no `LevelComponentSet` has been accepted yet. Required fields are `level_id`, `source_level_component_set_ref`, `candidate_component_inventory_ref` when no accepted `LevelComponentSet` exists, `coupling_audit_report_refs`, `cohesion_audit_report_refs` when the decision depends on per-component organization evidence, `verdict`, `changed_component_refs`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, `new_abstraction_component_refs`, `integration_test_refs`, `rationale_evidence_refs`, and `defer_to_prototype_risk`.\n- Rule: `CouplingDecision.verdict` is exactly one of `merge_components`, `introduce_abstraction_component`, or `no_change`. `merge_components` means the current-level component inventory collapses the affected component refs into one component. `introduce_abstraction_component` means the current-level component inventory adds a new abstraction component to decouple shared dependencies. `no_change` means coupling/cohesion evidence was considered and the current component inventory remains unchanged. A `no_change` verdict does not bypass `HaltRecord` or no-split evidence when existing derivation or halt rules require them.\n- Rule: Evidence-bearing halt rule: a level halts only when candidate splits earn granularity under none of the `halt_basis` options: `clarify contracts`, `reduce accidental coupling`, `expose a design challenge`, `improve evidence`, `lower meaningful risk`. The `halt_basis` field records option-level evidence showing why each listed option was unsatisfied. The canonical halt record path is `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-halt-record.md`. The implementation-pipeline orchestrator checks this rule procedurally per `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § `#### Phase 6 halt-state transition gate`.\n- Rule: `HaltRecord` required fields are `halt_basis`, `component_candidates_considered`, `evidence_refs`, and `residual_risk_refs`. `component_candidates_considered` is required and lists considered or rejected split candidates so the halt is replayable.\n- Rule: an interacting component pair listed in `component_pair_refs[]` without corresponding `integration_test_refs` is not non-applicability and must not be silently skipped; it halts with violation code `integration_test_missing` and requires replayable missing-pair evidence naming which pair and what expected coverage was missing.\n- Rule: Halt verdict-resolution rule: halt-by-omission must not override a split or revise verdict. An accepted halt that conflicts with an overlay verdict cites the conflicting overlay verdict evidence and the evidence justifying halt; unresolved accepted conflicts are recorded as Decision Recording entries named `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual`.\n- Rule: Process-tree review: after Step 6c completes and before Phase 7, dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)`. The operator-owned expected-process manifest and process-tree audit #2 must prove separate Step 6b and Step 6c invocations, timing order, Step 6b output index presence, Step 6b output paths, ACR-247 side-channel consumption evidence carried by the expected-process manifest for those outputs, test-contract alignment, prototype risk review, per-component code-quality, the derivation record or no-split / rejection subsection when a derivation trigger fires, `HaltRecord` or halt/no-split evidence when halt logic fires, procedural handoff evidence for Step 6c-discovered obligations, and `CouplingDecision` evidence with rerun/current `integration_test_refs` when coupling examination changes or confirms the current component inventory. Missing, malformed, mismatched, stale, late, wrong-scope side-file evidence, or a currentness mismatch under `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema` and § `Row-level re-verification` is blocking. A returned `BLOCKED`, `NEEDS_INPUT`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `STALE_REFUSAL`, or process-tree blocking row prevents Phase 7; the affected subtree is rerun or repaired before downstream consumption.\n- Rule: Phase 6 -> Phase 7 `PrototypeSwapRecord` boundary: this rule sits after Step 6c's process-tree review and before Phase 7; Phase 6 still has exactly three sub-steps, Step 6a, Step 6b, and Step 6c, so this is not a Step 6d. Before Phase 7 can advance or any optional PR-mode review can consume the diff, every WU or level scope with a level prototype must produce a `PrototypeSwapRecord` at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; a WU or level scope without a level prototype must place an explicit non-applicability statement at that same path, because a missing artifact is not equivalent to non-applicability. The record must contain `component_refs`, `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, `level_behavior_test_results`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, and `audit_overlay_refs`, with `level_id` and `prototype_ref` additional to the six ticket fields, plus a terminal-state field whose allowed values are `consumed`, `non-applicable`, and `superseded`. Each `audit_overlay_refs` entry must identify the artifact path and the verdict or currentness evidence proving the artifact applies to the swapped component inventory. The canonical implementation-pipeline workflow forbids the implementation-pipeline orchestrator from advancing past the Phase 7 readiness checks on a diff that has no `PrototypeSwapRecord` or explicit non-applicability statement at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; Phase 7 dispatch cannot advance and the optional review operator cannot consume that diff. This rule authorizes no new operators and no new workflow file, and it does not define overlay scheduling, which belongs to sibling NES-270. This prohibition is implemented by the `Pre-dispatch swap-record gate` in `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § Phase 7, which the orchestrator agent executes procedurally. Parallel prototype code cannot remain as shadow implementation unless the proposal at the level scope has named retained ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n- Boundary pointer: same-layer component-pair integration-test evidence is upstream evidence consumed before swap readiness and is distinct from `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, and `level_behavior_test_results`.\n\n## Phase 7 - Optional PR-Mode Review Delegation\n\nThe implementation-pipeline orchestrator runs readiness checks, then calls `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py` directly for optional PR-mode CodeRabbit review. The script owns @mention triggering, trigger-ack polling, GitHub review-state interpretation, review/comment normalization, and per-comment body persistence. The repository-level `coderabbit` label is only an installation marker; applying it to a PR suppresses CodeRabbit and is not a trigger.\n\n- Readiness checks: inherited prototype proof tests, integration-test evidence for same-layer interacting component pairs, and `PrototypeSwapRecord` / explicit non-applicability.\n- Enablement primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py is-enabled ${repo}`. Exit `1` is a clean skip. Other nonzero exits block Phase 7.\n- Trigger mode criterion: use `incremental` for normal PR-review flow and for re-runs after child fix commits. Use `full` only for code-audit or mass-cleanup workflows whose declared review target is whole files rather than the latest diff, such as file-by-file risk-assessment cleanup passes.\n- Loop primitive: `~/ai/tools/coderabbit_review_driver.py review-loop ${repo} ${pr_num} --worktree-path ${worktree_path} --mode ${coderabbit_trigger_mode}`.\n- The driver owns the full loop: trigger or auto-skip an already-pending trigger, wait for ack, enforce the 300-second minimum cadence between loop-owned polls, dispatch one fixer invocation per actionable in-diff comment, preserve each structured outcome, push fixed commits, post reply files, trigger incremental follow-up reviews, and continue until terminal.\n- `terminal_reason=approved` is CodeRabbit success.\n- `terminal_reason=no_value_provided` is accepted convergence when every actionable in-diff comment in the latest iteration was assessed `review_provided_value: false`.\n- `needs_caller_decision=true` surfaces valuable `rejected` or `deferred` per-comment outcomes to the root caller without re-triggering.\n- The orchestrator must not compose its own trigger/poll/dispatch/retrigger loop, must not use inline `gh pr view ... statusCheckRollup` polling, must not inline CodeRabbit comment bodies, and must not synthesize an empty review pass.\n- If the pipeline creates or reuses a PR, it writes `${scratch_dir}/pr-url.txt`; Phase 9 reuses that URL instead of creating a duplicate PR.\n\n## Phase 8 - Post-Review Gates\n\n- These are the PR-opening gates on the actual diff.\n- Policy: dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-8)` after Phase 7 readiness and optional review delegation, before draft PR movement.\n- The Phase 8 gate-set inventory is test-audit review, multi-concern PR review, justification PR review, commit hygiene, Phase 8 actual-diff code-quality, proof-risk and validation-integrity runtime-claim transport where applicable, supported-surface inventory-resolution while ACR-286 remains open, currentness rechecks against prior joins, `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-8-join-manifest.json`, and process-tree audit #3.\n- Rule: the actual PR diff is the review target. Proposal-shaped evidence cannot satisfy actual-diff rows.\n- Rule: `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-8)` writes or verifies the Phase 8 join manifest at `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-8-join-manifest.json`, projects expected-process evidence, and requires process-tree audit #3 evidence. Rows carry at least `canonical_output_path`, `size`, `mtime`, `sha256`, `verdict_line`, and `verified_at`, plus the operator schema's caller mode, raw/normalized verdict, applicability, currentness, inventory-resolution, and blocking-status fields. Currentness rechecks against prior joins follow `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema`, § `Row-level re-verification`, and § `Full re-dispatch`.\n- Rule: the Phase 8 code-quality rows must carry actual-diff runtime-claim context under the Phase 3 `## Proof plan` rule. Validation-integrity and proof-risk remain required where that context makes them applicable.\n- Rule: if the multi-concern row says the diff should be split, split it before opening PRs.\n- Rule: preserve ACR-280 Phase 8 code-quality strategy selection. For current semantic Phase 8 code-quality `MEDIUM` or `HIGH`, choose MOVE-and-import and record `STRATEGY_PHASE8_CODE_QUALITY_MOVE_AND_IMPORT`, in-place file-decomposition and record `STRATEGY_PHASE8_CODE_QUALITY_FILE_DECOMPOSITION`, in-WU remediation/helper extraction and record `STRATEGY_PHASE8_CODE_QUALITY_INWU`, or follow-up-ticket decomposition and record `STRATEGY_PHASE8_CODE_QUALITY_FOLLOWUP`. Follow-up tickets require current-WU narrowing and rerun or explicit `DECOMPOSED`; they never allow advance by themselves.\n- Rule: only a returned `PASS` from `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-8)` permits closure judge dispatch, draft PR creation, ticket comment work, or PR movement. `BLOCKED`, `NEEDS_INPUT`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `STALE_REFUSAL`, stale rows, missing output paths, malformed manifests, or process-tree blocking findings prevent draft PR creation until the affected subtree is rerun or repaired.\n- Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the root handles it through `~/ai/conventions/agent-questions-and-session-graph.md`. Do not advance this phase, write code, post review output, or open a PR from work that depends on the unanswered question.\n- Phase 8 is the review and capture layer that precedes draft PR creation; it is not followed by a human branch-local review fallback before Phase 9.\n- Phase 8 capture documentation: after `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-8)` returns `PASS` with process-tree audit #3 evidence cleared, the orchestrator dispatches the `gpt-xhigh` closure judge and records `actual_story_points` and `actual_capture_method` as an orchestrator-owned closure artifact before Phase 9. The enum is `closer-best-effort | wall-time-derived | unmeasured`, the point set is `1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 40, 100`, and wall-time-derived is reserved as enum-only with no requirements for timers, durations, start/stop timestamps, or trace-derived time calculations.\n- Rule: ACR-125 capture must not dispatch `task=update-estimate` for actuals, must not write actuals to Linear `estimate`, and must not write actuals to Jira `customfield_10016`; live ticket estimate fields continue to hold the refined estimate.\n- Final verification-only behavior: Final does not recalculate the actual; it verifies the audit-history calibration block and `estimate_comparison_comment_ref`, then references the comparison in the close-comment.\n- Final halt-on-missing: on a missing/invalid/inconsistent calibration block or invalid `estimate_comparison_comment_ref`, Final halts and must not post the close-comment. Final verification accepts `<id | url | none>`, rejects `path`, and treats none valid only when `ticket_system: jira`. On success, Final close-comment includes inherited/refined/actual values inline on both backends.\n\n```worked example\nPhase 8 worked example\n\ninherited_story_point_estimate: 3\nrefined_story_point_estimate: 5\nactual_story_points: 8\nactual_capture_method: closer-best-effort\nestimate_delta_narrative: inherited=3; refined=5; actual=8; delta_refined_to_actual=3; over_2x_inherited=true\n\nsample comment body\n\nEstimate calibration\n\n- inherited_story_point_estimate: 3\n- refined_story_point_estimate: 5\n- actual_story_points: 8\n- actual_capture_method: closer-best-effort\n- actual_estimate_rationale: closer-best-effort: closure scope landed broader than the refined estimate assumed.\n- estimate_delta_narrative: inherited=3; refined=5; actual=8; delta_refined_to_actual=3; over_2x_inherited=true\n- audit_history: ${planning_dir}/audit-history.md\n```\n\n## Phase 9 - Draft PR\n\n- Routine automation step after all prior gates pass.\n- Rule: if Phase 7 wrote `${scratch_dir}/pr-url.txt`, Phase 9 verifies and reuses that PR instead of running `gh pr create` again.\n- Rule: open one draft PR per concern identified by multi-concern review.\n- Rule: respect dependency order for stacked work.\n- Rule: follow `~/ai/conventions/git.md`.\n- Rule: opening a **draft** PR is not Tier-3 by default.\n- Rule: **the title and body MUST be authored by `~/ai/agents/pr-writer.md`** — never hand-written by the orchestrator and never inlined into the `gh pr create` invocation. The writer enforces audience-and-content rules (no internal jargon, no commit-history sections, no closed-PR or local-planning-artifact references) that hand-written or templated bodies routinely violate. Pass the relevant phase artifacts (`problem-map.md`, `proposal.md`, `contract.md`, RCA evidence) as `context_files` so the writer has intent grounding without citing them in the body. For stacked PRs, supply `stack_parent_pr=<num>`; for any reference to merged work, supply `merged_refs=<list>`; when the selected ticket backend is Linear and the Linear key is known, supply `linear_issue_keys=<KEY>` so `pr-writer` emits the close-keyword footer itself.\n- Rule: RCA-track. The draft PR description includes before/after evidence for every Phase 0 red signal, whether a newly authored reproduction test or an existing failing test accepted as the reproduction signal: the red-run command and output or accepted failure evidence, paired with the matching post-fix green-run command and output recorded in Step 6c. Pass the evidence path(s) to `pr-writer` via `context_files` so they're folded into the body in audience-safe form.\n\n## Phase 10 - Promote To Ready-For-Review\n\n- Automated by the orchestrator once Phase 9 succeeds and the Phase 8 process-tree audit has cleared.\n- Rule: the orchestrator runs `gh pr ready <pr_number>` and records the promotion in `${scratch_dir}/pr-promoted.txt`. No human gate.\n\n## Gate Ownership\n\nSee `~/ai/conventions/gate-ownership.md` for the phase-by-phase table.\n\nRule of thumb:\n- the implementation-pipeline human gates are (1) the Phase 2.5 problem-map review and (2) legitimate NEEDS_INPUT new-value-question surfacing per `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`\n- every other phase belongs to a model gate or to automation\n- the proposal is reviewed by the risk gate, not by an extra human approval pass\n- Phase 9 (draft PR) and Phase 10 (promotion) are both automated by the orchestrator\n\n## Tiered Approval\n\nAnything with user-visible side effects follows `~/ai/workflows/tiered-approval.md`.\n\nPractical default:\n- drafting code is not Tier-3\n- editing files is not Tier-3\n- running tests is not Tier-3\n- opening **draft** PRs is not Tier-3\n- public promotion and other outward-facing actions follow the tiered-approval workflow\n\n## Pause For Refactor\n\nThis section owns the pause state model for existing-code refactor work discovered during an implementation WU. For the strategy boundary, cite `conventions/feature-development-workflow.md` `## Refactoring out of scope`; do not restate that rule elsewhere in this workflow.\n\n- trigger: an auditor, reviewer, or implementation step identifies existing-code structural refactor work that is outside touched-file/component ownership, or inside touched ownership but too large for the current WU, and material to continuing safely.\n- pause evidence: record the current ticket id, branch, head SHA, worktree path, dirty status, failing or residual report paths, and the reason the refactor is outside touched ownership or requires decomposition.\n- ticket/backend action: use the selected ticket backend to comment the pause evidence on the current ticket and apply the available pause/blocking status or label; if no status exists, use a clear blocking comment plus link relation.\n- refactor-ticket filing: file a linked refactor-ticket with the bounded structural target, preserved-behavior requirement, evidence paths, and relationship to the paused WU.\n- ACR-179 routing: run the refactor-ticket through the ACR-179 refactoring route, using `workflows/refactoring.md` and `agents/refactoring-orchestrator.md`.\n- resume: resume the paused WU only after the refactor-ticket is closed, superseded, or explicitly dispositioned by the active manager flavor with recorded evidence.\n- touched-file/component gate failures: this pause path does not alter touched-file/component ownership; current-WU `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` code-quality findings still revise, remediate, or decompose under the existing LOW-only rules.\n\n## Decision Recording\n\n<!-- INTENTIONAL: \"deferred\" here is a decision-recording state category, not an unwired workflow stub. -->\nIf a phase is skipped, narrowed, deferred, terminated for non-positive value, sent back to research on invalidated assumptions, or accepted with a named unverifiable residual risk that does not collapse the approved net-value case under the supported-surface termination rule:\n- record it in `${worktree_path}/DECISIONS.md` or an explicitly supplied project equivalent\n- record who made the decision\n- record why the change was accepted\n- record what risk is being accepted\n- record what evidence caused the termination or research re-entry\n\nFor Phase 6 firstness, do not create a new residual class. If a named test-verification risk remains unverified, record it in `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`. If only historical or process-provenance evidence is unverifiable and the approved net-value case still holds, record the decision only as `accepted with a named unverifiable residual risk` in `${worktree_path}/DECISIONS.md` or an explicitly supplied project equivalent.\n\nAccepted halt residuals and accepted `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual` entries follow this same Decision Recording rule when they do not collapse the approved net-value case.\n\nDecision Recording, generic residual recording, and Phase 7 / Phase 8 residual / value-zero review language do NOT authorize residual acceptance of Phase 4 code-quality, Phase 6 prototype-risk, or per-component code-quality `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` verdicts. Those gate verdicts revise, remediate, or decompose per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Disposition policy` and § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`; they are never recorded as residuals. Generic residual recording remains valid for non-gate residual classes only (bounded test-verification residuals in `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, process-provenance uncertainty per the Phase 6 firstness paragraph above, accepted halt residuals, accepted `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual` entries, and Phase 8 value-zero termination evidence).\n\nWithout that record, the phase was not skipped correctly.\n\n## Orchestrator\n\nThis pipeline is dispatched by the `implementation-pipeline-orchestrator` (`gpt-xhigh`) operator at `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`. The orchestrator is the only delegated actor that walks a Work Unit through every phase, dispatches each leaf operator via the `agents` CLI, runs the three required process-tree audits, and enforces the violation-escalation policy autonomously (including Tier-1 main-branch rewinds when a violation is detected). Inlining the orchestrator role into the root conversation is itself a workflow violation because it removes the orchestration decisions from `agents trace --json` and prevents process-tree-auditor from auditing the workflow as a whole.\n\n## Adjacent References\n\n- `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`\n- `~/ai/models/roles.md`\n- `~/ai/workflows/agents-cli.md`\n- `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`\n- `~/ai/conventions/git.md`\n- `~/ai/agents/coderabbit-operator.md`\n- `~/ai/workflows/pr-review.md`\n- `~/ai/workflows/tiered-approval.md`\n- `~/ai/conventions/no-backwards-compatibility.md`\n- `~/ai/conventions/no-deferred-stubs.md`\n- `~/ai/conventions/gate-ownership.md`\n- `~/ai/conventions/audit-history.md`","lineStart":500,"lineEnd":652,"totalLines":652,"truncated":false}},"title":"../../../../ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","time":{"start":1780803012761,"end":1780803012830}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_081ae533f4033423016a24e5c4a98481978c02eac067df3ec0"}},"id":"prt_ea0218891001P2KMG7TcfltqXE","sessionID":"ses_15fdf386fffe9fkXvMGWNEHLbs","messageID":"msg_ea02170eb0010We2gZSRNkAbkT"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780803012849,"sessionID":"ses_15fdf386fffe9fkXvMGWNEHLbs","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_vRU3okjbVmfR78Gdk4xDaNYB","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","offset":300,"limit":170},"output":"<path>/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n300: - Rule: bugs / ambiguities / drift / test-gap discoveries are handled per `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Discoveries during Phase 2.5. New tracker tickets (JIRA or Linear, per project) that block the current WU halt the orchestrator until user disposition is given.\n301: - Rule: Phase 2.5 cannot run in lean mode itself — it is the input to mode selection for downstream phases. The artifacts must all be produced. The size of the artifact for a particular sub-step may be small (e.g. coverage inventory for a one-file change is short), but the artifact is required.\n302: - Gate: human. **This remains the first of the two surviving human gates.** The user confirms (a) the problem map names the right terrain, (b) the risk profile's per-surface verdicts are accepted, (c) any blocking-ticket disposition is settled, and (d) the WU is workable in the implementation pipeline at all (vs. needs deferral to a prototype) before proposal work begins.\n303: \n304: ### Defer to prototype\n305: \n306: Sometimes Phase 2.5 surfaces evidence that the WU is not workable in the implementation pipeline yet. Signals:\n307: \n308: - The risk profile rolls up HIGH on **most** surfaces (not one or two — most). Exhaustive mode would expand the WU into multiple WUs.\n309: - The duplicates inventory (sub-step 2.5.4) finds a sprawling parallel-systems landscape outside touched-file/component ownership, or inside touched ownership but too large for one coherent WU, that the WU cannot navigate without architectural decisions or decomposition.\n310: - The lifecycle map (sub-step 2.5.2) cannot be drawn — too much of the touched process is operational knowledge the codebase doesn't capture.\n311: - The coverage inventory (sub-step 2.5.1) finds the touched surface so under-specified that characterization tests would themselves be a multi-WU effort before the actual change can be designed.\n312: - The cross-language trace (sub-step 2.5.5) reveals the surface crosses boundaries with implicit contracts in so many places that the change-path entropy alone scores HIGH.\n313: \n314: <!-- INTENTIONAL: \"defer to prototype\" is the user-visible Phase 2.5 disposition, not an unwired future-ticket stub; the implementation-pipeline orchestrator wires the dispatch, ticket marking, cross-link comment, and halt in its Phase 2.5 human-gate branch. -->\n315: When two or more of these fire, the Phase 2.5 human gate surfaces options: `proceed in exhaustive mode (the WU is just big)`, `defer to prototype (framing is too unclear; build a prototype to clarify, then re-ticket from its dossier)`, `terminate WU (the work is wrong-shaped; abandon)`. Choosing **defer to prototype** dispatches `~/ai/agents/prototype-orchestrator.md` per `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` with the WU's question as the prototype's question and the WU's `${ticket_id}` (`jira_issue_key` or `linear_issue_key`, per project) as `defer_source`. Before the implementation orchestrator halts the deferred WU, it marks the original ticket as deferred with backend-specific evidence: the Linear path applies `deferred-to-prototype` through `linear-operator task=apply-labels create_missing=true replace=false`; the Jira path attempts `jira-operator task=transition target_status=Blocked`, and if `Blocked` is unavailable the backend fallback is a comment-only deferred marker. Sprint/cycle removal is attempted only where a declared backend operation exists; with the current Linear/Jira operator surfaces it is unsupported and recorded as `fallback:operationally-manual` in the immediate comment and execution evidence. The immediate comment cross-links the prototype identity or tracker key, the prototype dossier path or pending dossier path, the deferred marker, and the sprint/cycle removal fallback. When the prototype later publishes a prototype-test PR, spawned implementation tickets must inherit the prototype-test PR URL, branch, test paths/node IDs, marker reason, ticket mapping, and acceptance criterion from `~/ai/conventions/prototype-pending-tests.md` § `Carry-forward to implementation`: remove the `prototype-pending:` markers, make the inherited prototype proof tests pass, and preserve original assertions unless a traceable strictly stronger equivalent supersession is recorded. The implementation orchestrator halts the deferred WU; the prototype's dossier later ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n316: \n317: worked example: a WU defers at Phase 2.5, so the original Linear ticket receives the `deferred-to-prototype` label, or the original Jira ticket moves to `Blocked` with a comment-only backend fallback when that transition is unavailable. The cross-link comment names the prototype identity or tracker, records that sprint/cycle removal is unsupported through current operators unless a backend adds the operation, and points to the pending dossier. The prototype dossier ships with spawned tickets and an approved original-ticket disposition. If the dossier says `close-as-superseded`, prototype P4 files the spawned tickets first, then closes the original as superseded where the backend supports that status and writes backend-specific links/comments: Jira uses supported issue links such as `Cloners`, while Linear uses parent/sub-issue where possible or durable comments. If backend support is missing, the fallback is recorded rather than silently claiming the operation happened.\n318: \n319: Deferring to prototype is not failure. It is the recognition that the implementation pipeline's gates exist to enforce known structure on a clear plan, and the work currently has neither.\n320: \n321: ## Phase 3 - Proposal\n322: \n323: - Artifact: `proposals/NN-*.md`\n324: - Inputs: approved `problem map`, coverage inventory, lifecycle map, entrypoints, duplicates, cross-language trace, halt records and halt residual evidence when present, and **risk profile** from Phase 2.5; any research from Phases 1-2.\n325: - Role: propose design, scope, architecture, tradeoffs, `Forbidden behaviors`, a test-intent track, and the specific current-state risk the change reduces. `Forbidden behaviors` is behavior-forbidding only; it must not state work-narrowing exclusions that bypass touched-file/component ownership. **Do not** implement.\n326: - Rules: the proposal must be reviewable on its own terms. \"We will figure it out during implementation\" is not sufficient design. The proposer does not write its own risk reports.\n327: - Rule: **mode is per-surface, not per-ticket.** Read `risk/NN-risk-profile.md` and produce a per-surface mode list at the top of the proposal: which surfaces are HIGH (exhaustive content required), which are MEDIUM (lean with explicit MEDIUM-axis callouts), which are LOW (lean). Apply the artifact requirements named in `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Pipeline mode.\n328: - Rule: when the risk profile names duplicate systems on the touched surface, the proposal must explicitly address whether the WU cascades the change to all duplicates, consolidates them, or accepts the divergence. \"Cascade later\" is not addressed.\n329: - Rule: when approved Phase 2.5 inputs include `HaltRecord` evidence or halt residuals, the proposal must state whether it preserves, revises, invalidates, or pays down those prior residuals, including `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual`.\n330: - Rules: include a supported-surface track covering deployment mode, customer cohort, adjacent public or user-reachable paths, blast-radius notes for unchanged adjacent paths, migration path, rollback path, and observability.\n331: - Rules: include a qualitative net-value statement. State whether the proposal clearly reduces a concrete current-state risk on the current supported surface and whether that reduction clearly outweighs the added blast radius plus the migration and rollback burden.\n332: - Rules: name those inputs from the artifact fields. Existing-state risk comes from `known risky or brittle behavior already present` plus `current supported and user-reachable paths through the surface`; change blast radius comes from `adjacent surfaces within the blast radius` plus `adjacent public or user-reachable paths`; migration cost comes from `migration path` plus `rollback path`.\n333: - Rules: include a short assumption register. Each assumption must name the evidence it relies on and what source or observation would invalidate it. If research already drafted a register for this touched surface, Phase 3 validates and narrows it into the approved assumption register in `proposals/NN-*.md`. Do not keep a competing second register.\n334: - Rules: include a test-intent track. For each expected test or test group, name the change risk or verification risk, intended behavior or acceptance condition, selected level (`unit`, `component`, `particular-integration`, or `end-to-end`), fixture source or fixture application point, assumption-register link if the behavior depends on an assumption, expected observable signal, and any residual risk the test will not verify.\n335: - Rule: when the test-intent item is `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification, the test-intent track encodes lifecycle `WRITE` eval-spec authoring at `evals/<slug>/eval.md`, not pytest test files or one-off verifier scripts.\n336: - Rule: when predecessor evidence supplies inherited prototype proof tests, the Phase 3 test-intent track must list each inherited prototype test path or node ID as a required test-intent item and carry forward the no-silent-drop / strictly stronger equivalent supersession rule from `~/ai/conventions/prototype-pending-tests.md` § `Carry-forward to implementation`.\n337: - Rules: include a mandatory `## Proof plan` section with exactly these parse anchors:\n338:   - `**Runtime claim**: <one or more sentences naming the runtime behavior asserted by the fix>`\n339:   - `**Proof method**: <description of validation surface(s) that will exercise the runtime claim>`\n340:   - `**Evidence-class match**: <explicit statement that the proof method's evidence class matches the runtime claim's required evidence class>`\n341:   The proof plan is a proposal artifact, not a self-certification. Runtime-artifact-bound claims must name the runtime-artifact evidence class that will validate them; proxy evidence must be scoped to proxy-layer claims or paired with matching runtime-artifact proof.\n342: - Gate: no default human approval step. The proposal advances only by passing Phase 4.\n343: \n344: Phase 3 proposals MUST include a `## Estimate refinement` parse anchor with one fenced `yaml` block. The proposer's outputs contract includes `refined_story_point_estimate` and `estimate_delta_rationale`, and the fenced block is the only parse surface the orchestrator reads:\n345: \n346: ```yaml\n347: refined_story_point_estimate: 13\n348: estimate_delta_rationale: \"One sentence explaining the refinement.\"\n349: inherited_story_point_estimate: 5\n350: estimate_source: \"layer-3-slice\"\n351: estimate_delta_flag:\n352:   inherited: 5\n353:   refined: 13\n354:   over_2x: true\n355:   rationale: \"13 is greater than 2 * 5, so Phase 4 receives the delta as a scope-risk signal.\"\n356: ```\n357: \n358: After Phase 3 artifact verification and before Phase 4 prompt composition, the orchestrator dispatches `${ticket_operator}` with `task=update-estimate` and the parsed estimate inputs. The canonical prompt path is `${scratch_dir}/prompts/${wu_lower}-phase-3-update-estimate.md`; the canonical log path is `${scratch_dir}/logs/${wu_lower}-phase-3-update-estimate.log`. The Phase 4 scope-risk prompt receives `estimate_delta_flag`, including whether the refined value is `>2x` the inherited value (`true`, `false`, or `unknown`).\n359: \n360: ## Phase 4 - Risk Gates (required; apply-gate-set)\n361: \n362: Run the risk gate on the proposal artifact, the `problem map`, the supported-surface track, and the Phase 2.5 risk profile, not on hand-waved intent. The orchestrator composes one `apply-gate-set` prompt and dispatches `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-4)` after Phase 3 proposal and estimate update, before Phase 5 hookpoint research.\n363: \n364: - Artifacts represented by the gate-set: `risk/NN-audit.md`, `risk/NN-scope.md`, `risk/NN-shortcut.md`, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, `risk/NN-proof-risk.md`, Phase 4 code-quality outputs, `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-4-join-manifest.json`, expected-process evidence, process-tree audit #1, and optional bootstrap-exception ratification.\n365: - `audit-risk`: presence, contracts, migrations, test-intent track, fixture source, residual-risk artifact, and other checklist obligations.\n366: - `scope-risk`: does the proposal stay within the stated scope.\n367: - `shortcut-risk`: do proposed shortcuts defeat the underlying purpose.\n368: - `supported-surface-risk`: does the proposal reduce risk on the current supported surface it claims to target, using the approved `problem map`, supported-surface track, net-value statement, assumption register, and **risk profile**, including adjacent public or user-reachable paths, deployment mode, migration, rollback, and observability.\n369: - `proof-risk`: does the proposal's `## Proof plan` map each runtime claim to a proof method whose evidence class exercises that claim. It remains a distinct row or an explicit ACR-285 inventory-resolution row with equivalent blocking semantics.\n370: - `code-quality`: Phase 4 pipeline-callable code-quality over current WU evidence remains part of the Phase 4 gate-set. Only `LOW` passes unless a valid bootstrap-exception row ratifies a non-LOW aggregate under `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Bootstrap exception`.\n371: - Rule: `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-4)` writes or verifies the canonical Phase 4 join manifest at `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-4-join-manifest.json`, projects expected-process evidence, and requires process-tree audit #1 evidence. Rows carry at least `canonical_output_path`, `size`, `mtime`, `sha256`, `verdict_line`, and `verified_at`, plus the operator schema's caller mode, raw/normalized verdict, applicability, currentness, ratification, inventory-resolution, and blocking-status fields. Currentness keys and row-level reuse follow `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema` and § `Row-level re-verification`.\n372: - Rule: the bootstrap-exception parser remains orchestrator-owned. The orchestrator parses the proposal `## Bootstrap exception declaration`, parses the matching DECISIONS heading and convention citation, and passes ratification evidence into the gate-set. The operator writes the row; it does not become the authority for the proposer/orchestrator four-condition declaration.\n373: - Rule: in **lean mode** (per `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Pipeline mode) the supported-surface gate may pass at LOW with a single-paragraph supported-surface track and the audit gate may pass at LOW without per-AC residual entries. In **exhaustive mode** the gates require full evidence.\n374: - Rule: supported-surface termination is an orthogonal dimension from the LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH verdict. Evaluate it first and in this order: invalidated assumption that breaks the current problem framing -> return to research and resume at Phase 2.5; otherwise non-positive value on the current supported surface -> terminate the work. A `LOW` supported-surface verdict with a non-positive value signal still terminates. Only when no termination signal fires does the LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH verdict control the next step.\n375: - Rule: when no termination signal fires, any current, well-formed semantic `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` proposal-risk report means revise the proposal and rerun the Phase 4 gate-set from clean state. Do not keep old `LOW` reports after a substantive proposal revision.\n376: - Rule: preserve ACR-280 strategy selection. Supported-surface non-LOW records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SUPPORTED_SURFACE_INWU` or `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SUPPORTED_SURFACE_FOLLOWUP` when those classifications apply; scope decomposition records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SCOPE_DECOMPOSED`; code-quality non-LOW that is not ratified by bootstrap exception records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_INWU`, `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_HELPER_EXTRACTION`, `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_MOVE_AND_IMPORT`, in-place file-decomposition, or follow-up-ticket decomposition according to the current finding shape.\n377: - Rule: if proposal revision and risk review enter a second round, create or update audit history under `~/ai/conventions/audit-history.md`. Track prior-finding closure/regression, new findings, oscillation classification, decompose-trigger status, watch signals, and the current `continue` / `apply` / `decompose` determination.\n378: - Rule: if hard revise/review triggers do not decide `continue`, `apply`, or `decompose`, use the decision-agent dispatch pattern in `~/ai/conventions/audit-history.md` before the orchestrator calls the loop.\n379: - Rule: if a proposer claims it \"re-ran the risks itself,\" ignore that claim and rerun or reverify through the actual Phase 4 gate-set.\n380: - Gate: model. The risk gate is the proposal review.\n381: \n382: ## Phase 4.5 - Alignment (optional; governance layer only)\n383: \n384: - Use only when the project explicitly has a governance or philosophy layer that needs a directional check.\n385: - Artifact: `alignment/NN-*.md`\n386: - Role: check the proposal against governance, patterns, and philosophy and return `ALIGNED`, `MISALIGNED`, or `NEEDS_REVISION`.\n387: - Rules: alignment is directional, not exhaustive. It does not replace the audit gate. If the proposal changes materially after alignment feedback, re-run Phase 4 as well.\n388: - Gate: model. `ALIGNED` is required when this phase is enabled.\n389: \n390: ## Phase 5 - Hookpoint Research\n391: \n392: - Run only after the proposal has cleared risk, and alignment if alignment applies.\n393: - Artifact: `research/NN-hookpoints.md`\n394: - Role: map the approved proposal onto the existing codebase and find reusable pieces, extension points, conflicting systems, and deletion candidates.\n395: - Rule: identify anything parallel that should be merged into or removed instead of re-created. The Phase 2.5 duplicates inventory (`research/NN-duplicates.md`) is the input — Phase 5 confirms whether the planned change cascades, consolidates, or diverges per the proposal's decision.\n396: - Rule: in **exhaustive mode** for any surface scored HIGH on language-fragmentation or change-path-entropy, dispatch the `code-tracer` operator (`~/ai/agents/code-tracer.md`) to produce the cross-language readers/callers/contracts graph for that surface. Hookpoints derived from a single-language grep are insufficient when the surface crosses boundaries.\n397: - Rule: in **lean mode**, the four required sections (reuse / extension / conflicting / deletion candidates) are required as headers; sections may be one line or \"none observed\" but must be present.\n398: - Rule: All implementation branch work follows `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`; parallelism does not change the requirement.\n399: - Rule: if hookpoint research shows the approved `problem map` or assumption register is wrong, stop and return to research; resume at Phase 2.5 with an updated `problem map` before implementation continues. Returning to Phase 2.5 re-engages the problem-map human gate.\n400: - Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the orchestrator classifies procedural vs new-value; only new-value questions reach the root.\n401: - Gate: model. The orchestrator advances autonomously to Phase 6 once the hookpoints artifact is non-empty, contains the four required sections (reuse points / extension points / conflicting systems / deletion candidates), and does not invalidate the approved problem map or assumption register.\n402: \n403: ## Phase 6 - Implementation (required; test/code separation)\n404: \n405: Implementation has three sub-steps.\n406: The test writer and the code writer must be different agent invocations.\n407: That rule is load-bearing: if the same agent writes both, the tests mirror the implementation instead of validating the contract.\n408: Step 6a defines the level outer contract; during Step 6c, once passing evidence exists, post-prototype internal component contract derivation may be triggered.\n409: When Step 6c's post-prototype derivation accepts internal components, current-layer integration tests for interactions between those component pairs are required as part of the same derivation evidence.\n410: Rule: Step 6c may discover procedural obligations during implementation, but discovery does not relax the different-invocation rule; procedural tests still go through a separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation.\n411: After Step 6c has passing level evidence, the derivation trigger logic decides whether the level derives accepted internal component contracts, records an evidence-bearing halt/no-split when a trigger fires but no split earns granularity, or proceeds to Phase 7 unchanged when neither trigger arm fires.\n412: \n413: - Rule: Phase 6 firstness evidence is the Phase 6 process-tree review plus companion artifacts. The companion artifacts are the expected-process manifest, Step 6b prompt/log, Step 6c prompt/log, Step 6b output index, Step 6b output paths, and evidence that Step 6c consumed those outputs. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, that consumption evidence is the sibling `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` projection workflow output carried in the expected-process manifest; field detail belongs to that workflow and `process-tree-auditor`. A commit marker is optional supporting evidence only. A PR-diff test file is not required firstness evidence.\n414: - Convention: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/` resolves under the same per-run scratch directory supplied by the invoking workflow, matching the `scratch_dir` input pattern used by `agents/test-audit-gate.md` and `agents/red-phase-gate.md`.\n415: - Rule: Phase 6 consumes the approved `research/NN-problem-map.md`, approved `proposals/NN-*.md` including its test-intent track and assumption register, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and `research/NN-hookpoints.md`.\n416: - Rule: Coupling/cohesion role split. `coupling-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against integrations between components at the current recursion layer, using component pairs and their current-layer integration evidence. `cohesion-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against individual components at the current recursion layer, using per-component organization evidence to decide whether the component should remain as-is, be broken out through the existing recursive child-entry path, or contribute evidence to the current-level component-set decision. The auditors produce evidence; Phase 6 owns the component-set decision.\n417: - Rule: RCA-track. The red signals produced or accepted in Phase 0 are inputs to the Phase 3 test-intent track and to Phase 6b. Step 6b carries them forward (existing tests satisfy firstness when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk and source). Step 6c is not complete until each pre-fix red signal from Phase 0 runs green against post-fix HEAD.\n418: - Rule: RCA-track evidence is **verbatim output, not summary**. The red run is captured by reverting the planned-change product file(s) to the branch's base (`origin/main` or the stacked parent), running each newly authored reproduction test or accepted existing failing test command/node ID, and pasting the failure block into the Phase 9 evidence artifact. The green run is captured by restoring the fix and re-running the same signal, pasting the pass block. \"Tests passed\" is not evidence; the actual test-runner or browser-runner command output is.\n419: - Rule: Recursive child entry. A child recursion level enters `level-open` only after an audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord` exists for that child framing. Existing overlay and audit machinery supplies the review evidence through `audit_overlay_refs`; this workflow rule does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n420: - Rule: Recursive parent exit. A parent level may transition into `child-levels-open` only after each candidate child has been considered for framing. This rule does not define halt criteria; halt criteria belong to the sibling halt-rule WU.\n421: - Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse integration tests. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same level-scoped integration test semantics repeat for that child level's own `level_id`; integration-test evidence is not a child-entry condition or gate, and `ChildLevelFramingRecord` remains the child-entry condition.\n422: - Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same Step 6a contract, Step 6b test-first, Step 6c implementation, procedural-test, swap, and audit semantics apply at that child's `level_id` where those semantics exist, with continuous-overlay review evidence carried through `audit_overlay_refs` for the audited framing record.\n423: - Rule: if Step 6a, 6b, or 6c uncovers evidence that invalidates an approved assumption or shows the touched surface differs materially from the approved `problem map`, stop implementation and return to research; resume at Phase 2.5 before more code or tests are written.\n424: - Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the root handles it through `~/ai/conventions/agent-questions-and-session-graph.md`. Do not advance this phase, write code, post review output, or open a PR from work that depends on the unanswered question.\n425: \n426: ### Step 6a - Define contract\n427: \n428: - Owner: orchestrator\n429: - Produces: the level outer contract: schemas, signatures, commands, interface boundaries, explicit behavioral assumptions, fixture application points, and test-intent handoff\n430: - Rule: the contract must be clear enough that another agent can write tests from it without seeing implementation code.\n431: - Rule: the contract must preserve every change risk or verification risk, selected test level, fixture source, assumption-register link, and expected observable signal from the approved proposal test-intent track.\n432: - Rule: Step 6a does not author internal component contracts; those can only be derived from later prototype evidence when the Post-prototype internal contract derivation rule in Step 6c is triggered.\n433: - Rule: `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is Step 6a contract text for recursive child entry, not a standalone schema file or operator output. Required fields, in order:\n434:   1. `parent_level_id`\n435:   2. `source_component_implementation_ref`\n436:   3. `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`\n437:   4. `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`\n438:   5. `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`\n439:   6. `framing_rationale_evidence_refs`\n440:   7. `audit_overlay_refs`\n441:   8. `parent_artifact_currency_refs`\n442: - Rule: `parent_artifact_currency_refs` is load-bearing for currency audit. It records whether parent-side artifacts named by `source_component_implementation_ref`, `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`, `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`, and `framing_rationale_evidence_refs` are current for child entry; if any parent-side artifact is substantively revised, the `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is stale until refreshed, superseded, or accepted as residual risk.\n443: - Rule: `audit_overlay_refs` point to the existing overlay review evidence for the audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord`; this cross-reference does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n444: \n445: ### Step 6b - Encode tests first\n446: \n447: - Inputs: level outer contract, approved proposal test-intent track, approved `problem map`, **approved `risk/NN-risk-profile.md`**, characterization tests already produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 for any uncovered behaviors on the touched surface, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and hookpoint research.\n448: - Rule: the test writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6c.\n449: - Rule: the test writer does **not** see the implementation.\n450: - Rule: in **lean mode**, smoke + AC coverage is sufficient; residuals are named but not blocking. In **exhaustive mode**, per-named-risk + per-AC + property-based / mutation / fuzz coverage is required for surfaces scored HIGH on `coverage-gap` or `behavioral-ambiguity`; residual tests are blocking unless explicitly accepted in `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`. Per-surface mode applies independently.\n451: - Rule: characterization tests produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 are inputs, not duplicates. Step 6b adds tests for the **new** behavior the WU introduces; characterization tests stay in place to guard against regression of the existing behavior.\n452: - Rule: tests encode intended behavior from the contract and proposal test-intent track before Step 6c writes product code.\n453: - Rule: Step 6b tests follow `~/ai/conventions/testing.md`; that canonical rule governs test authorship for setup placement, setup swapping, and cross-module setup.\n454: - Rule: for `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification routes, Step 6b authors a WRITE-state eval spec instead of test files. The output is `evals/<slug>/eval.md` plus `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` may be referenced only as a prototype boundary when applicable, not as a reason to turn this route into prototype or pytest work.\n455: - Rule: the structural-verification eval-spec route inherits the binding `~/ai/conventions/evals.md` prohibition: no `tools/<wu>-verify/<anything>.py`, no `tests/test_*.py`, no pytest imports or fixtures, and no pytest-shaped assertion code.\n456: - Rule: every test or test group carries a risk annotation naming the risk it reduces, the selected level, and the proposal or assumption-register source. If a test verifies an assumption, the annotation says so.\n457: - Rule: firstness applies per named risk, selected level, and test group from the approved test-intent track. Existing tests satisfy this rule only when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk, selected level, and proposal or assumption-register source.\n458: - Rule: pre-existing code without prior tests is not required to prove retroactive firstness. Current-work behavior changes and current-work risk reduction are not grandfathered.\n459: - Rule: Recursive Step 6b carry-through maps `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, every emitted test group identifier, and each residual entry or residual reference for a child level to the same `level_id`. Preserve the fixture source or fixture application point in the Step 6b output index so the child-level application point remains traceable.\n460: - Rule: when a named risk cannot be verified by the test set, produce `risk/NN-test-residuals.md` with the residual class (`combinatorial/path-state`, `bounded-model`, `integration-hidden`, `emergent-interaction`, `temporal/concurrency`, or `generator/search-budget`), technique attempted or considered (`property-based`, `fuzzing`, `mutation`, `symbolic`, `model-checking`, `chaos`, or `graph`), scope, budget or bound, result, remaining residual, invalidating inputs, and whether the residual changes the net-value case.\n461: - Output: test files in the project test roots, with risk annotations on every test or test group.\n462: - Output: for structural-verification eval-spec routes, WRITE-state eval specs at `evals/<slug>/eval.md`, with lifecycle and finding contract fields required by `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`.\n463: - Output: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, listing every proposal test-intent item, named risk, selected level, source, emitted test file, test group, or eval-spec artifact path, residual entry, documented non-applicability reason, and listing implementation-discovered procedural obligations with their emitted procedural tests or residual entries.\n464: - Output-index fields: approved proposal path, contract path, approved `problem map` path, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md` path, hookpoint research path, Step 6b prompt path, Step 6b log path, `level_id` when recursive Phase 6 work emits level-scoped artifacts, each test-intent item, named risk, selected level, proposal or assumption-register source, emitted test file path, eval-spec path, and test/eval/test-group identifier, residual entry path when the item maps to `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, documented non-applicability reason when no test or eval spec is emitted, declared fixture source or fixture application point, procedural obligation, Step 6c evidence that discovered the obligation, emitted procedural test file path and test or test-group identifier when a procedural test was authored, or procedural residual entry path when no procedural test is emitted; residual class when no procedural test is emitted. The path `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md` remains the Step 6b output index path; recursive rows and artifact identifiers carry `level_id` and use `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` when a string identifier is needed.\n465: - Rule: when predecessor evidence supplies inherited prototype `test_paths_or_node_ids`, the Step 6b output index must map every inherited prototype entry to a Step 6b-authored production-tree test, an existing passing production-tree test, or a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index. A missing mapping is not non-applicability.\n466: - Output-index fields for accepted internal components: integration-test intent at selected level `particular-integration`, the applicable `layer_level_id` / `level_id` scope, the `LevelComponentSet` artifact path, and later `integration_test_refs` when Step 6c completes concrete pair coverage after accepted internal components and interactions are known.\n467: - Rule: When Step 6c records a `CouplingDecision`, the Step 6b output index also lists the `coupling_decision_ref`, affected `level_id`, source `LevelComponentSet` or candidate inventory reference, and any rerun `integration_test_refs` that prove the emitted tests still match the current component inventory.\n468: - Output: `risk/NN-test-residuals.md` when any named risk remains unverified.\n469: - Rule: Phase 6 prototype risk review is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype and level behavior tests pass. The required row preserves `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-risk.md`, verdict vocabulary `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, and `HIGH`, and the existing allow/block semantics before downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord`, or Phase 7 readiness consumes the prototype. This row remains distinct from `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` exploratory prototype `dossier/risk-profile.md` lifecycle.\n\n(Showing lines 300-469 of 652. Use offset=470 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- Rule: bugs / ambiguities / drift / test-gap discoveries are handled per `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Discoveries during Phase 2.5. New tracker tickets (JIRA or Linear, per project) that block the current WU halt the orchestrator until user disposition is given.\n- Rule: Phase 2.5 cannot run in lean mode itself — it is the input to mode selection for downstream phases. The artifacts must all be produced. The size of the artifact for a particular sub-step may be small (e.g. coverage inventory for a one-file change is short), but the artifact is required.\n- Gate: human. **This remains the first of the two surviving human gates.** The user confirms (a) the problem map names the right terrain, (b) the risk profile's per-surface verdicts are accepted, (c) any blocking-ticket disposition is settled, and (d) the WU is workable in the implementation pipeline at all (vs. needs deferral to a prototype) before proposal work begins.\n\n### Defer to prototype\n\nSometimes Phase 2.5 surfaces evidence that the WU is not workable in the implementation pipeline yet. Signals:\n\n- The risk profile rolls up HIGH on **most** surfaces (not one or two — most). Exhaustive mode would expand the WU into multiple WUs.\n- The duplicates inventory (sub-step 2.5.4) finds a sprawling parallel-systems landscape outside touched-file/component ownership, or inside touched ownership but too large for one coherent WU, that the WU cannot navigate without architectural decisions or decomposition.\n- The lifecycle map (sub-step 2.5.2) cannot be drawn — too much of the touched process is operational knowledge the codebase doesn't capture.\n- The coverage inventory (sub-step 2.5.1) finds the touched surface so under-specified that characterization tests would themselves be a multi-WU effort before the actual change can be designed.\n- The cross-language trace (sub-step 2.5.5) reveals the surface crosses boundaries with implicit contracts in so many places that the change-path entropy alone scores HIGH.\n\n<!-- INTENTIONAL: \"defer to prototype\" is the user-visible Phase 2.5 disposition, not an unwired future-ticket stub; the implementation-pipeline orchestrator wires the dispatch, ticket marking, cross-link comment, and halt in its Phase 2.5 human-gate branch. -->\nWhen two or more of these fire, the Phase 2.5 human gate surfaces options: `proceed in exhaustive mode (the WU is just big)`, `defer to prototype (framing is too unclear; build a prototype to clarify, then re-ticket from its dossier)`, `terminate WU (the work is wrong-shaped; abandon)`. Choosing **defer to prototype** dispatches `~/ai/agents/prototype-orchestrator.md` per `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` with the WU's question as the prototype's question and the WU's `${ticket_id}` (`jira_issue_key` or `linear_issue_key`, per project) as `defer_source`. Before the implementation orchestrator halts the deferred WU, it marks the original ticket as deferred with backend-specific evidence: the Linear path applies `deferred-to-prototype` through `linear-operator task=apply-labels create_missing=true replace=false`; the Jira path attempts `jira-operator task=transition target_status=Blocked`, and if `Blocked` is unavailable the backend fallback is a comment-only deferred marker. Sprint/cycle removal is attempted only where a declared backend operation exists; with the current Linear/Jira operator surfaces it is unsupported and recorded as `fallback:operationally-manual` in the immediate comment and execution evidence. The immediate comment cross-links the prototype identity or tracker key, the prototype dossier path or pending dossier path, the deferred marker, and the sprint/cycle removal fallback. When the prototype later publishes a prototype-test PR, spawned implementation tickets must inherit the prototype-test PR URL, branch, test paths/node IDs, marker reason, ticket mapping, and acceptance criterion from `~/ai/conventions/prototype-pending-tests.md` § `Carry-forward to implementation`: remove the `prototype-pending:` markers, make the inherited prototype proof tests pass, and preserve original assertions unless a traceable strictly stronger equivalent supersession is recorded. The implementation orchestrator halts the deferred WU; the prototype's dossier later ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n\nworked example: a WU defers at Phase 2.5, so the original Linear ticket receives the `deferred-to-prototype` label, or the original Jira ticket moves to `Blocked` with a comment-only backend fallback when that transition is unavailable. The cross-link comment names the prototype identity or tracker, records that sprint/cycle removal is unsupported through current operators unless a backend adds the operation, and points to the pending dossier. The prototype dossier ships with spawned tickets and an approved original-ticket disposition. If the dossier says `close-as-superseded`, prototype P4 files the spawned tickets first, then closes the original as superseded where the backend supports that status and writes backend-specific links/comments: Jira uses supported issue links such as `Cloners`, while Linear uses parent/sub-issue where possible or durable comments. If backend support is missing, the fallback is recorded rather than silently claiming the operation happened.\n\nDeferring to prototype is not failure. It is the recognition that the implementation pipeline's gates exist to enforce known structure on a clear plan, and the work currently has neither.","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","text":"- Rule: bugs / ambiguities / drift / test-gap discoveries are handled per `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Discoveries during Phase 2.5. New tracker tickets (JIRA or Linear, per project) that block the current WU halt the orchestrator until user disposition is given.\n- Rule: Phase 2.5 cannot run in lean mode itself — it is the input to mode selection for downstream phases. The artifacts must all be produced. The size of the artifact for a particular sub-step may be small (e.g. coverage inventory for a one-file change is short), but the artifact is required.\n- Gate: human. **This remains the first of the two surviving human gates.** The user confirms (a) the problem map names the right terrain, (b) the risk profile's per-surface verdicts are accepted, (c) any blocking-ticket disposition is settled, and (d) the WU is workable in the implementation pipeline at all (vs. needs deferral to a prototype) before proposal work begins.\n\n### Defer to prototype\n\nSometimes Phase 2.5 surfaces evidence that the WU is not workable in the implementation pipeline yet. Signals:\n\n- The risk profile rolls up HIGH on **most** surfaces (not one or two — most). Exhaustive mode would expand the WU into multiple WUs.\n- The duplicates inventory (sub-step 2.5.4) finds a sprawling parallel-systems landscape outside touched-file/component ownership, or inside touched ownership but too large for one coherent WU, that the WU cannot navigate without architectural decisions or decomposition.\n- The lifecycle map (sub-step 2.5.2) cannot be drawn — too much of the touched process is operational knowledge the codebase doesn't capture.\n- The coverage inventory (sub-step 2.5.1) finds the touched surface so under-specified that characterization tests would themselves be a multi-WU effort before the actual change can be designed.\n- The cross-language trace (sub-step 2.5.5) reveals the surface crosses boundaries with implicit contracts in so many places that the change-path entropy alone scores HIGH.\n\n<!-- INTENTIONAL: \"defer to prototype\" is the user-visible Phase 2.5 disposition, not an unwired future-ticket stub; the implementation-pipeline orchestrator wires the dispatch, ticket marking, cross-link comment, and halt in its Phase 2.5 human-gate branch. -->\nWhen two or more of these fire, the Phase 2.5 human gate surfaces options: `proceed in exhaustive mode (the WU is just big)`, `defer to prototype (framing is too unclear; build a prototype to clarify, then re-ticket from its dossier)`, `terminate WU (the work is wrong-shaped; abandon)`. Choosing **defer to prototype** dispatches `~/ai/agents/prototype-orchestrator.md` per `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` with the WU's question as the prototype's question and the WU's `${ticket_id}` (`jira_issue_key` or `linear_issue_key`, per project) as `defer_source`. Before the implementation orchestrator halts the deferred WU, it marks the original ticket as deferred with backend-specific evidence: the Linear path applies `deferred-to-prototype` through `linear-operator task=apply-labels create_missing=true replace=false`; the Jira path attempts `jira-operator task=transition target_status=Blocked`, and if `Blocked` is unavailable the backend fallback is a comment-only deferred marker. Sprint/cycle removal is attempted only where a declared backend operation exists; with the current Linear/Jira operator surfaces it is unsupported and recorded as `fallback:operationally-manual` in the immediate comment and execution evidence. The immediate comment cross-links the prototype identity or tracker key, the prototype dossier path or pending dossier path, the deferred marker, and the sprint/cycle removal fallback. When the prototype later publishes a prototype-test PR, spawned implementation tickets must inherit the prototype-test PR URL, branch, test paths/node IDs, marker reason, ticket mapping, and acceptance criterion from `~/ai/conventions/prototype-pending-tests.md` § `Carry-forward to implementation`: remove the `prototype-pending:` markers, make the inherited prototype proof tests pass, and preserve original assertions unless a traceable strictly stronger equivalent supersession is recorded. The implementation orchestrator halts the deferred WU; the prototype's dossier later ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n\nworked example: a WU defers at Phase 2.5, so the original Linear ticket receives the `deferred-to-prototype` label, or the original Jira ticket moves to `Blocked` with a comment-only backend fallback when that transition is unavailable. The cross-link comment names the prototype identity or tracker, records that sprint/cycle removal is unsupported through current operators unless a backend adds the operation, and points to the pending dossier. The prototype dossier ships with spawned tickets and an approved original-ticket disposition. If the dossier says `close-as-superseded`, prototype P4 files the spawned tickets first, then closes the original as superseded where the backend supports that status and writes backend-specific links/comments: Jira uses supported issue links such as `Cloners`, while Linear uses parent/sub-issue where possible or durable comments. If backend support is missing, the fallback is recorded rather than silently claiming the operation happened.\n\nDeferring to prototype is not failure. It is the recognition that the implementation pipeline's gates exist to enforce known structure on a clear plan, and the work currently has neither.\n\n## Phase 3 - Proposal\n\n- Artifact: `proposals/NN-*.md`\n- Inputs: approved `problem map`, coverage inventory, lifecycle map, entrypoints, duplicates, cross-language trace, halt records and halt residual evidence when present, and **risk profile** from Phase 2.5; any research from Phases 1-2.\n- Role: propose design, scope, architecture, tradeoffs, `Forbidden behaviors`, a test-intent track, and the specific current-state risk the change reduces. `Forbidden behaviors` is behavior-forbidding only; it must not state work-narrowing exclusions that bypass touched-file/component ownership. **Do not** implement.\n- Rules: the proposal must be reviewable on its own terms. \"We will figure it out during implementation\" is not sufficient design. The proposer does not write its own risk reports.\n- Rule: **mode is per-surface, not per-ticket.** Read `risk/NN-risk-profile.md` and produce a per-surface mode list at the top of the proposal: which surfaces are HIGH (exhaustive content required), which are MEDIUM (lean with explicit MEDIUM-axis callouts), which are LOW (lean). Apply the artifact requirements named in `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Pipeline mode.\n- Rule: when the risk profile names duplicate systems on the touched surface, the proposal must explicitly address whether the WU cascades the change to all duplicates, consolidates them, or accepts the divergence. \"Cascade later\" is not addressed.\n- Rule: when approved Phase 2.5 inputs include `HaltRecord` evidence or halt residuals, the proposal must state whether it preserves, revises, invalidates, or pays down those prior residuals, including `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual`.\n- Rules: include a supported-surface track covering deployment mode, customer cohort, adjacent public or user-reachable paths, blast-radius notes for unchanged adjacent paths, migration path, rollback path, and observability.\n- Rules: include a qualitative net-value statement. State whether the proposal clearly reduces a concrete current-state risk on the current supported surface and whether that reduction clearly outweighs the added blast radius plus the migration and rollback burden.\n- Rules: name those inputs from the artifact fields. Existing-state risk comes from `known risky or brittle behavior already present` plus `current supported and user-reachable paths through the surface`; change blast radius comes from `adjacent surfaces within the blast radius` plus `adjacent public or user-reachable paths`; migration cost comes from `migration path` plus `rollback path`.\n- Rules: include a short assumption register. Each assumption must name the evidence it relies on and what source or observation would invalidate it. If research already drafted a register for this touched surface, Phase 3 validates and narrows it into the approved assumption register in `proposals/NN-*.md`. Do not keep a competing second register.\n- Rules: include a test-intent track. For each expected test or test group, name the change risk or verification risk, intended behavior or acceptance condition, selected level (`unit`, `component`, `particular-integration`, or `end-to-end`), fixture source or fixture application point, assumption-register link if the behavior depends on an assumption, expected observable signal, and any residual risk the test will not verify.\n- Rule: when the test-intent item is `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification, the test-intent track encodes lifecycle `WRITE` eval-spec authoring at `evals/<slug>/eval.md`, not pytest test files or one-off verifier scripts.\n- Rule: when predecessor evidence supplies inherited prototype proof tests, the Phase 3 test-intent track must list each inherited prototype test path or node ID as a required test-intent item and carry forward the no-silent-drop / strictly stronger equivalent supersession rule from `~/ai/conventions/prototype-pending-tests.md` § `Carry-forward to implementation`.\n- Rules: include a mandatory `## Proof plan` section with exactly these parse anchors:\n  - `**Runtime claim**: <one or more sentences naming the runtime behavior asserted by the fix>`\n  - `**Proof method**: <description of validation surface(s) that will exercise the runtime claim>`\n  - `**Evidence-class match**: <explicit statement that the proof method's evidence class matches the runtime claim's required evidence class>`\n  The proof plan is a proposal artifact, not a self-certification. Runtime-artifact-bound claims must name the runtime-artifact evidence class that will validate them; proxy evidence must be scoped to proxy-layer claims or paired with matching runtime-artifact proof.\n- Gate: no default human approval step. The proposal advances only by passing Phase 4.\n\nPhase 3 proposals MUST include a `## Estimate refinement` parse anchor with one fenced `yaml` block. The proposer's outputs contract includes `refined_story_point_estimate` and `estimate_delta_rationale`, and the fenced block is the only parse surface the orchestrator reads:\n\n```yaml\nrefined_story_point_estimate: 13\nestimate_delta_rationale: \"One sentence explaining the refinement.\"\ninherited_story_point_estimate: 5\nestimate_source: \"layer-3-slice\"\nestimate_delta_flag:\n  inherited: 5\n  refined: 13\n  over_2x: true\n  rationale: \"13 is greater than 2 * 5, so Phase 4 receives the delta as a scope-risk signal.\"\n```\n\nAfter Phase 3 artifact verification and before Phase 4 prompt composition, the orchestrator dispatches `${ticket_operator}` with `task=update-estimate` and the parsed estimate inputs. The canonical prompt path is `${scratch_dir}/prompts/${wu_lower}-phase-3-update-estimate.md`; the canonical log path is `${scratch_dir}/logs/${wu_lower}-phase-3-update-estimate.log`. The Phase 4 scope-risk prompt receives `estimate_delta_flag`, including whether the refined value is `>2x` the inherited value (`true`, `false`, or `unknown`).\n\n## Phase 4 - Risk Gates (required; apply-gate-set)\n\nRun the risk gate on the proposal artifact, the `problem map`, the supported-surface track, and the Phase 2.5 risk profile, not on hand-waved intent. The orchestrator composes one `apply-gate-set` prompt and dispatches `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-4)` after Phase 3 proposal and estimate update, before Phase 5 hookpoint research.\n\n- Artifacts represented by the gate-set: `risk/NN-audit.md`, `risk/NN-scope.md`, `risk/NN-shortcut.md`, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, `risk/NN-proof-risk.md`, Phase 4 code-quality outputs, `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-4-join-manifest.json`, expected-process evidence, process-tree audit #1, and optional bootstrap-exception ratification.\n- `audit-risk`: presence, contracts, migrations, test-intent track, fixture source, residual-risk artifact, and other checklist obligations.\n- `scope-risk`: does the proposal stay within the stated scope.\n- `shortcut-risk`: do proposed shortcuts defeat the underlying purpose.\n- `supported-surface-risk`: does the proposal reduce risk on the current supported surface it claims to target, using the approved `problem map`, supported-surface track, net-value statement, assumption register, and **risk profile**, including adjacent public or user-reachable paths, deployment mode, migration, rollback, and observability.\n- `proof-risk`: does the proposal's `## Proof plan` map each runtime claim to a proof method whose evidence class exercises that claim. It remains a distinct row or an explicit ACR-285 inventory-resolution row with equivalent blocking semantics.\n- `code-quality`: Phase 4 pipeline-callable code-quality over current WU evidence remains part of the Phase 4 gate-set. Only `LOW` passes unless a valid bootstrap-exception row ratifies a non-LOW aggregate under `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Bootstrap exception`.\n- Rule: `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-4)` writes or verifies the canonical Phase 4 join manifest at `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-4-join-manifest.json`, projects expected-process evidence, and requires process-tree audit #1 evidence. Rows carry at least `canonical_output_path`, `size`, `mtime`, `sha256`, `verdict_line`, and `verified_at`, plus the operator schema's caller mode, raw/normalized verdict, applicability, currentness, ratification, inventory-resolution, and blocking-status fields. Currentness keys and row-level reuse follow `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema` and § `Row-level re-verification`.\n- Rule: the bootstrap-exception parser remains orchestrator-owned. The orchestrator parses the proposal `## Bootstrap exception declaration`, parses the matching DECISIONS heading and convention citation, and passes ratification evidence into the gate-set. The operator writes the row; it does not become the authority for the proposer/orchestrator four-condition declaration.\n- Rule: in **lean mode** (per `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Pipeline mode) the supported-surface gate may pass at LOW with a single-paragraph supported-surface track and the audit gate may pass at LOW without per-AC residual entries. In **exhaustive mode** the gates require full evidence.\n- Rule: supported-surface termination is an orthogonal dimension from the LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH verdict. Evaluate it first and in this order: invalidated assumption that breaks the current problem framing -> return to research and resume at Phase 2.5; otherwise non-positive value on the current supported surface -> terminate the work. A `LOW` supported-surface verdict with a non-positive value signal still terminates. Only when no termination signal fires does the LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH verdict control the next step.\n- Rule: when no termination signal fires, any current, well-formed semantic `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` proposal-risk report means revise the proposal and rerun the Phase 4 gate-set from clean state. Do not keep old `LOW` reports after a substantive proposal revision.\n- Rule: preserve ACR-280 strategy selection. Supported-surface non-LOW records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SUPPORTED_SURFACE_INWU` or `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SUPPORTED_SURFACE_FOLLOWUP` when those classifications apply; scope decomposition records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SCOPE_DECOMPOSED`; code-quality non-LOW that is not ratified by bootstrap exception records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_INWU`, `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_HELPER_EXTRACTION`, `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_MOVE_AND_IMPORT`, in-place file-decomposition, or follow-up-ticket decomposition according to the current finding shape.\n- Rule: if proposal revision and risk review enter a second round, create or update audit history under `~/ai/conventions/audit-history.md`. Track prior-finding closure/regression, new findings, oscillation classification, decompose-trigger status, watch signals, and the current `continue` / `apply` / `decompose` determination.\n- Rule: if hard revise/review triggers do not decide `continue`, `apply`, or `decompose`, use the decision-agent dispatch pattern in `~/ai/conventions/audit-history.md` before the orchestrator calls the loop.\n- Rule: if a proposer claims it \"re-ran the risks itself,\" ignore that claim and rerun or reverify through the actual Phase 4 gate-set.\n- Gate: model. The risk gate is the proposal review.\n\n## Phase 4.5 - Alignment (optional; governance layer only)\n\n- Use only when the project explicitly has a governance or philosophy layer that needs a directional check.\n- Artifact: `alignment/NN-*.md`\n- Role: check the proposal against governance, patterns, and philosophy and return `ALIGNED`, `MISALIGNED`, or `NEEDS_REVISION`.\n- Rules: alignment is directional, not exhaustive. It does not replace the audit gate. If the proposal changes materially after alignment feedback, re-run Phase 4 as well.\n- Gate: model. `ALIGNED` is required when this phase is enabled.\n\n## Phase 5 - Hookpoint Research\n\n- Run only after the proposal has cleared risk, and alignment if alignment applies.\n- Artifact: `research/NN-hookpoints.md`\n- Role: map the approved proposal onto the existing codebase and find reusable pieces, extension points, conflicting systems, and deletion candidates.\n- Rule: identify anything parallel that should be merged into or removed instead of re-created. The Phase 2.5 duplicates inventory (`research/NN-duplicates.md`) is the input — Phase 5 confirms whether the planned change cascades, consolidates, or diverges per the proposal's decision.\n- Rule: in **exhaustive mode** for any surface scored HIGH on language-fragmentation or change-path-entropy, dispatch the `code-tracer` operator (`~/ai/agents/code-tracer.md`) to produce the cross-language readers/callers/contracts graph for that surface. Hookpoints derived from a single-language grep are insufficient when the surface crosses boundaries.\n- Rule: in **lean mode**, the four required sections (reuse / extension / conflicting / deletion candidates) are required as headers; sections may be one line or \"none observed\" but must be present.\n- Rule: All implementation branch work follows `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`; parallelism does not change the requirement.\n- Rule: if hookpoint research shows the approved `problem map` or assumption register is wrong, stop and return to research; resume at Phase 2.5 with an updated `problem map` before implementation continues. Returning to Phase 2.5 re-engages the problem-map human gate.\n- Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the orchestrator classifies procedural vs new-value; only new-value questions reach the root.\n- Gate: model. The orchestrator advances autonomously to Phase 6 once the hookpoints artifact is non-empty, contains the four required sections (reuse points / extension points / conflicting systems / deletion candidates), and does not invalidate the approved problem map or assumption register.\n\n## Phase 6 - Implementation (required; test/code separation)\n\nImplementation has three sub-steps.\nThe test writer and the code writer must be different agent invocations.\nThat rule is load-bearing: if the same agent writes both, the tests mirror the implementation instead of validating the contract.\nStep 6a defines the level outer contract; during Step 6c, once passing evidence exists, post-prototype internal component contract derivation may be triggered.\nWhen Step 6c's post-prototype derivation accepts internal components, current-layer integration tests for interactions between those component pairs are required as part of the same derivation evidence.\nRule: Step 6c may discover procedural obligations during implementation, but discovery does not relax the different-invocation rule; procedural tests still go through a separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation.\nAfter Step 6c has passing level evidence, the derivation trigger logic decides whether the level derives accepted internal component contracts, records an evidence-bearing halt/no-split when a trigger fires but no split earns granularity, or proceeds to Phase 7 unchanged when neither trigger arm fires.\n\n- Rule: Phase 6 firstness evidence is the Phase 6 process-tree review plus companion artifacts. The companion artifacts are the expected-process manifest, Step 6b prompt/log, Step 6c prompt/log, Step 6b output index, Step 6b output paths, and evidence that Step 6c consumed those outputs. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, that consumption evidence is the sibling `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` projection workflow output carried in the expected-process manifest; field detail belongs to that workflow and `process-tree-auditor`. A commit marker is optional supporting evidence only. A PR-diff test file is not required firstness evidence.\n- Convention: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/` resolves under the same per-run scratch directory supplied by the invoking workflow, matching the `scratch_dir` input pattern used by `agents/test-audit-gate.md` and `agents/red-phase-gate.md`.\n- Rule: Phase 6 consumes the approved `research/NN-problem-map.md`, approved `proposals/NN-*.md` including its test-intent track and assumption register, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and `research/NN-hookpoints.md`.\n- Rule: Coupling/cohesion role split. `coupling-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against integrations between components at the current recursion layer, using component pairs and their current-layer integration evidence. `cohesion-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against individual components at the current recursion layer, using per-component organization evidence to decide whether the component should remain as-is, be broken out through the existing recursive child-entry path, or contribute evidence to the current-level component-set decision. The auditors produce evidence; Phase 6 owns the component-set decision.\n- Rule: RCA-track. The red signals produced or accepted in Phase 0 are inputs to the Phase 3 test-intent track and to Phase 6b. Step 6b carries them forward (existing tests satisfy firstness when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk and source). Step 6c is not complete until each pre-fix red signal from Phase 0 runs green against post-fix HEAD.\n- Rule: RCA-track evidence is **verbatim output, not summary**. The red run is captured by reverting the planned-change product file(s) to the branch's base (`origin/main` or the stacked parent), running each newly authored reproduction test or accepted existing failing test command/node ID, and pasting the failure block into the Phase 9 evidence artifact. The green run is captured by restoring the fix and re-running the same signal, pasting the pass block. \"Tests passed\" is not evidence; the actual test-runner or browser-runner command output is.\n- Rule: Recursive child entry. A child recursion level enters `level-open` only after an audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord` exists for that child framing. Existing overlay and audit machinery supplies the review evidence through `audit_overlay_refs`; this workflow rule does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n- Rule: Recursive parent exit. A parent level may transition into `child-levels-open` only after each candidate child has been considered for framing. This rule does not define halt criteria; halt criteria belong to the sibling halt-rule WU.\n- Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse integration tests. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same level-scoped integration test semantics repeat for that child level's own `level_id`; integration-test evidence is not a child-entry condition or gate, and `ChildLevelFramingRecord` remains the child-entry condition.\n- Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same Step 6a contract, Step 6b test-first, Step 6c implementation, procedural-test, swap, and audit semantics apply at that child's `level_id` where those semantics exist, with continuous-overlay review evidence carried through `audit_overlay_refs` for the audited framing record.\n- Rule: if Step 6a, 6b, or 6c uncovers evidence that invalidates an approved assumption or shows the touched surface differs materially from the approved `problem map`, stop implementation and return to research; resume at Phase 2.5 before more code or tests are written.\n- Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the root handles it through `~/ai/conventions/agent-questions-and-session-graph.md`. Do not advance this phase, write code, post review output, or open a PR from work that depends on the unanswered question.\n\n### Step 6a - Define contract\n\n- Owner: orchestrator\n- Produces: the level outer contract: schemas, signatures, commands, interface boundaries, explicit behavioral assumptions, fixture application points, and test-intent handoff\n- Rule: the contract must be clear enough that another agent can write tests from it without seeing implementation code.\n- Rule: the contract must preserve every change risk or verification risk, selected test level, fixture source, assumption-register link, and expected observable signal from the approved proposal test-intent track.\n- Rule: Step 6a does not author internal component contracts; those can only be derived from later prototype evidence when the Post-prototype internal contract derivation rule in Step 6c is triggered.\n- Rule: `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is Step 6a contract text for recursive child entry, not a standalone schema file or operator output. Required fields, in order:\n  1. `parent_level_id`\n  2. `source_component_implementation_ref`\n  3. `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`\n  4. `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`\n  5. `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`\n  6. `framing_rationale_evidence_refs`\n  7. `audit_overlay_refs`\n  8. `parent_artifact_currency_refs`\n- Rule: `parent_artifact_currency_refs` is load-bearing for currency audit. It records whether parent-side artifacts named by `source_component_implementation_ref`, `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`, `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`, and `framing_rationale_evidence_refs` are current for child entry; if any parent-side artifact is substantively revised, the `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is stale until refreshed, superseded, or accepted as residual risk.\n- Rule: `audit_overlay_refs` point to the existing overlay review evidence for the audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord`; this cross-reference does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n\n### Step 6b - Encode tests first\n\n- Inputs: level outer contract, approved proposal test-intent track, approved `problem map`, **approved `risk/NN-risk-profile.md`**, characterization tests already produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 for any uncovered behaviors on the touched surface, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and hookpoint research.\n- Rule: the test writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6c.\n- Rule: the test writer does **not** see the implementation.\n- Rule: in **lean mode**, smoke + AC coverage is sufficient; residuals are named but not blocking. In **exhaustive mode**, per-named-risk + per-AC + property-based / mutation / fuzz coverage is required for surfaces scored HIGH on `coverage-gap` or `behavioral-ambiguity`; residual tests are blocking unless explicitly accepted in `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`. Per-surface mode applies independently.\n- Rule: characterization tests produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 are inputs, not duplicates. Step 6b adds tests for the **new** behavior the WU introduces; characterization tests stay in place to guard against regression of the existing behavior.\n- Rule: tests encode intended behavior from the contract and proposal test-intent track before Step 6c writes product code.\n- Rule: Step 6b tests follow `~/ai/conventions/testing.md`; that canonical rule governs test authorship for setup placement, setup swapping, and cross-module setup.\n- Rule: for `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification routes, Step 6b authors a WRITE-state eval spec instead of test files. The output is `evals/<slug>/eval.md` plus `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` may be referenced only as a prototype boundary when applicable, not as a reason to turn this route into prototype or pytest work.\n- Rule: the structural-verification eval-spec route inherits the binding `~/ai/conventions/evals.md` prohibition: no `tools/<wu>-verify/<anything>.py`, no `tests/test_*.py`, no pytest imports or fixtures, and no pytest-shaped assertion code.\n- Rule: every test or test group carries a risk annotation naming the risk it reduces, the selected level, and the proposal or assumption-register source. If a test verifies an assumption, the annotation says so.\n- Rule: firstness applies per named risk, selected level, and test group from the approved test-intent track. Existing tests satisfy this rule only when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk, selected level, and proposal or assumption-register source.\n- Rule: pre-existing code without prior tests is not required to prove retroactive firstness. Current-work behavior changes and current-work risk reduction are not grandfathered.\n- Rule: Recursive Step 6b carry-through maps `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, every emitted test group identifier, and each residual entry or residual reference for a child level to the same `level_id`. Preserve the fixture source or fixture application point in the Step 6b output index so the child-level application point remains traceable.\n- Rule: when a named risk cannot be verified by the test set, produce `risk/NN-test-residuals.md` with the residual class (`combinatorial/path-state`, `bounded-model`, `integration-hidden`, `emergent-interaction`, `temporal/concurrency`, or `generator/search-budget`), technique attempted or considered (`property-based`, `fuzzing`, `mutation`, `symbolic`, `model-checking`, `chaos`, or `graph`), scope, budget or bound, result, remaining residual, invalidating inputs, and whether the residual changes the net-value case.\n- Output: test files in the project test roots, with risk annotations on every test or test group.\n- Output: for structural-verification eval-spec routes, WRITE-state eval specs at `evals/<slug>/eval.md`, with lifecycle and finding contract fields required by `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`.\n- Output: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, listing every proposal test-intent item, named risk, selected level, source, emitted test file, test group, or eval-spec artifact path, residual entry, documented non-applicability reason, and listing implementation-discovered procedural obligations with their emitted procedural tests or residual entries.\n- Output-index fields: approved proposal path, contract path, approved `problem map` path, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md` path, hookpoint research path, Step 6b prompt path, Step 6b log path, `level_id` when recursive Phase 6 work emits level-scoped artifacts, each test-intent item, named risk, selected level, proposal or assumption-register source, emitted test file path, eval-spec path, and test/eval/test-group identifier, residual entry path when the item maps to `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, documented non-applicability reason when no test or eval spec is emitted, declared fixture source or fixture application point, procedural obligation, Step 6c evidence that discovered the obligation, emitted procedural test file path and test or test-group identifier when a procedural test was authored, or procedural residual entry path when no procedural test is emitted; residual class when no procedural test is emitted. The path `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md` remains the Step 6b output index path; recursive rows and artifact identifiers carry `level_id` and use `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` when a string identifier is needed.\n- Rule: when predecessor evidence supplies inherited prototype `test_paths_or_node_ids`, the Step 6b output index must map every inherited prototype entry to a Step 6b-authored production-tree test, an existing passing production-tree test, or a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index. A missing mapping is not non-applicability.\n- Output-index fields for accepted internal components: integration-test intent at selected level `particular-integration`, the applicable `layer_level_id` / `level_id` scope, the `LevelComponentSet` artifact path, and later `integration_test_refs` when Step 6c completes concrete pair coverage after accepted internal components and interactions are known.\n- Rule: When Step 6c records a `CouplingDecision`, the Step 6b output index also lists the `coupling_decision_ref`, affected `level_id`, source `LevelComponentSet` or candidate inventory reference, and any rerun `integration_test_refs` that prove the emitted tests still match the current component inventory.\n- Output: `risk/NN-test-residuals.md` when any named risk remains unverified.\n- Rule: Phase 6 prototype risk review is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype and level behavior tests pass. The required row preserves `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-risk.md`, verdict vocabulary `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, and `HIGH`, and the existing allow/block semantics before downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord`, or Phase 7 readiness consumes the prototype. This row remains distinct from `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` exploratory prototype `dossier/risk-profile.md` lifecycle.","lineStart":300,"lineEnd":469,"totalLines":652,"truncated":true}},"title":"../../../../ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","time":{"start":1780803012770,"end":1780803012837}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_081ae533f4033423016a24e5c4a98c819798a8d985893b3427"}},"id":"prt_ea021889d001Gy22fN9tUdeuh0","sessionID":"ses_15fdf386fffe9fkXvMGWNEHLbs","messageID":"msg_ea02170eb0010We2gZSRNkAbkT"}}
