{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780803021102,"sessionID":"ses_15fdf386fffe9fkXvMGWNEHLbs","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_zLZlBcugfmOvFWvkbj3bzJOX","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s11-gate/gates/diff.patch","offset":1,"limit":420},"output":"<path>/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s11-gate/gates/diff.patch</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: diff --git a/DECISIONS.md b/DECISIONS.md\n2: index 87da438..858bb6e 100644\n3: --- a/DECISIONS.md\n4: +++ b/DECISIONS.md\n5: @@ -1,5 +1,19 @@\n6:  # Project Decisions\n7:  \n8: +## D-S10B-VI-001-external-provider-schema-compatible-describe — validation-surface weakening ratification\n9: +\n10: +- **Source**: `planning/s10b-gate/code-quality/s10b/reports/validation-integrity-auditor.md` VI-001 / VI-s10b-001.\n11: +- **Decision**: Ratify the host DTO broadening for `ProviderConcurrency` as compatibility with the external provider protocol schema, not a relaxation of runtime safety. The external provider protocol permits provider-specific concurrency metadata, so `safe_for_parallel_invocation` and `state_locking` must remain optional while unknown concurrency keys are preserved as metadata.\n12: +- **Evidence**: `crates/oulipoly-provider/tests/client_invoke.rs::invoke_describe_accepts_schema_valid_freeform_concurrency_metadata`; `planning/s10b-gate/proposal.md` proof claim for schema-compatible describe; `planning/s10b-gate/contracts/s10b.contract.md` helper-shaped parser/validator declarations; `planning/s10b-gate/evidence/runtime-tests.log` isolated `cargo test --workspace`; live launch evidence in `/tmp/s10-e2e/final.log` and `/tmp/s10-e2e/final2.log` reached the external provider and returned `S10-EXTERNAL-OK`.\n13: +- **Revisit when**: the provider protocol schema makes concurrency fields strict again or introduces a separate typed concurrency capability contract.\n14: +\n15: +## D-S10B-VI-002-provider-ref-resume-external-launch — validation-surface weakening ratification\n16: +\n17: +- **Source**: `planning/s10b-gate/code-quality/s10b/reports/validation-integrity-auditor.md` VI-002 / VI-s10b-002.\n18: +- **Decision**: Ratify provider-ref resume bypassing legacy CLI resume-strategy validation and legacy default migration only when replacement provider-ref target validation succeeds. Provider-ref models are implemented by the external provider launch contract, so headless resume must launch externally with `known_provider_session_id` and recorded cwd instead of requiring a legacy provider `resume` block or running default migration/rotation; the target must still prove a resolved model, root provider implementation reference, valid provider index, and selected-provider identity agreement.\n19: +- **Evidence**: `src-tauri/src/run/resume/orchestration.rs::validate_provider_ref_headless_resume_target`; `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs::external_provider_resume_without_rotate_uses_external_launch_and_recorded_cwd`; `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/s10_external_launch_session.rs::external_launch_exit_session_populates_capture_and_resume_request`; `planning/s10b-gate/proposal.md` proof claims for provider-ref resume path, target validation, and recorded cwd; `planning/s10b-gate/contracts/s10b.contract.md` validator/predicate/formatter declarations for the provider-ref target invariant; `planning/s10b-gate/evidence/runtime-tests.log` isolated `cargo test --workspace`.\n20: +- **Revisit when**: provider-ref models gain a separate native resume capability distinct from external launch or when legacy migration becomes provider-ref aware.\n21: +\n22:  ## D-OC-VI-001-stdout-json-event-dual-shape — strict OpenCode capture support\n23:  \n24:  - **Source**: `planning/oc-gate/code-quality/oc/reports/validation-integrity-auditor.md` VI-001.\n25: diff --git a/crates/oulipoly-provider/src/client.rs b/crates/oulipoly-provider/src/client.rs\n26: index 97bcbf9..1d757b3 100644\n27: --- a/crates/oulipoly-provider/src/client.rs\n28: +++ b/crates/oulipoly-provider/src/client.rs\n29: @@ -3,7 +3,7 @@ use crate::error::{\n30:      check_contract_and_request, request_id_from,\n31:  };\n32:  use crate::generated::ProcessStatus;\n33: -use crate::process::{ByteLimit, ProcessCommand, ProcessLimits, ProcessRunner};\n34: +use crate::process::{ByteLimit, ProcessCommand, ProcessLimits, ProcessOutcome, ProcessRunner};\n35:  use crate::resolver::{\n36:      ProviderArtifactRef, ProviderResolveOptions, ProviderResolver, ResolvedProviderCommand,\n37:  };\n38: @@ -15,21 +15,48 @@ use std::ffi::OsString;\n39:  use std::sync::Mutex;\n40:  use std::time::Duration;\n41:  \n42: -pub use crate::process::CancellationToken;\n43: +pub use crate::process::{CancellationToken, ProcessSpawnObserver};\n44: +\n45: +/// Handshake (`describe` + `policy.evaluate`) timeout.\n46: +///\n47: +/// The handshake spawns the provider artifact, which may embed a scripting\n48: +/// runtime that must cold-start before it can answer `describe` /\n49: +/// `policy.evaluate`. Under machine load (many concurrent dispatches racing for\n50: +/// CPU) that cold start has been observed to take tens of seconds. The previous\n51: +/// 30s budget fired `host_timeout` mid-handshake, and because a transport\n52: +/// timeout is terminal the whole dispatch failed instead of completing.\n53: +///\n54: +/// 90s gives roughly 3x headroom over the observed worst case, so a genuinely\n55: +/// hung handshake still fails in bounded time (and the host can then rotate to\n56: +/// the next pool account rather than terminal-failing the dispatch).\n57: +const DEFAULT_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT: Duration = Duration::from_secs(90);\n58: +\n59: +/// Maximum gap between launch JSONL events before the host tears down the\n60: +/// provider process tree. The launch turn itself has no total deadline; real\n61: +/// agent turns can run for tens of minutes as long as the provider keeps\n62: +/// emitting events or heartbeat events within this 120s liveness window.\n63: +const DEFAULT_LAUNCH_HEARTBEAT_GAP: Duration = Duration::from_secs(120);\n64: +\n65: +/// Grace period between SIGTERM and SIGKILL when tearing down a timed-out or\n66: +/// cancelled provider process tree.\n67: +const DEFAULT_KILL_AFTER_GRACE: Duration = Duration::from_millis(100);\n68:  \n69:  #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n70:  pub struct ProviderTimeouts {\n71: +    /// Total timeout for non-launch handshake subcommands.\n72:      pub default: Duration,\n73: +    /// Maximum gap between launch stdout JSONL event lines.\n74:      pub launch: Duration,\n75: +    /// Grace period between SIGTERM and SIGKILL during teardown.\n76:      pub kill_after_grace: Duration,\n77:  }\n78:  \n79:  impl Default for ProviderTimeouts {\n80:      fn default() -> Self {\n81:          Self {\n82: -            default: Duration::from_secs(30),\n83: -            launch: Duration::from_secs(300),\n84: -            kill_after_grace: Duration::from_millis(100),\n85: +            default: DEFAULT_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT,\n86: +            launch: DEFAULT_LAUNCH_HEARTBEAT_GAP,\n87: +            kill_after_grace: DEFAULT_KILL_AFTER_GRACE,\n88:          }\n89:      }\n90:  }\n91: @@ -56,6 +83,7 @@ pub struct ProviderClientOptions {\n92:      pub timeout: Duration,\n93:      pub cancellation: Option<CancellationToken>,\n94:      pub resolver: ProviderResolveOptions,\n95: +    pub spawn_observer: Option<ProcessSpawnObserver>,\n96:  }\n97:  \n98:  pub trait ProviderEnv {\n99: @@ -93,6 +121,7 @@ impl Default for ProviderClientOptions {\n100:              output_limits: ProviderOutputLimits::default(),\n101:              cancellation: None,\n102:              resolver: ProviderResolveOptions::default(),\n103: +            spawn_observer: None,\n104:          }\n105:      }\n106:  }\n107: @@ -105,6 +134,11 @@ impl ProviderClientOptions {\n108:          self\n109:      }\n110:  \n111: +    pub fn with_launch_heartbeat_gap(mut self, gap: Duration) -> Self {\n112: +        self.timeouts.launch = gap;\n113: +        self\n114: +    }\n115: +\n116:      pub fn with_kill_after_grace(mut self, duration: Duration) -> Self {\n117:          self.timeouts.kill_after_grace = duration;\n118:          self\n119: @@ -114,6 +148,11 @@ impl ProviderClientOptions {\n120:          self.cancellation = cancellation;\n121:          self\n122:      }\n123: +\n124: +    pub fn with_spawn_observer(mut self, observer: Option<ProcessSpawnObserver>) -> Self {\n125: +        self.spawn_observer = observer;\n126: +        self\n127: +    }\n128:  }\n129:  \n130:  #[derive(Debug)]\n131: @@ -163,14 +202,7 @@ impl ProviderClient {\n132:          I: ProviderEnv,\n133:      {\n134:          let envelope = self.invoke_json(subcommand, request, envs)?;\n135: -        serde_json::from_value(envelope[\"result\"].clone()).map_err(|error| {\n136: -            ProviderClientError::protocol(\n137: -                HostErrorKind::SchemaInvalidResponse,\n138: -                subcommand,\n139: -                request_id_from(&envelope),\n140: -                ProviderDiagnostics::with_description(error.to_string()),\n141: -            )\n142: -        })\n143: +        deserialize_typed_result(subcommand, envelope)\n144:      }\n145:  \n146:      pub fn invoke_json<I>(\n147: @@ -184,118 +216,33 @@ impl ProviderClient {\n148:      {\n149:          let request_id = request_id_from(&request);\n150:          let registry = SchemaRegistry::new();\n151: -        registry\n152: -            .validate_request(subcommand, &request)\n153: -            .map_err(|error| map_request_validation_error(error, subcommand, request_id.clone()))?;\n154: +        validate_json_request(&registry, subcommand, &request, request_id.clone())?;\n155:          let resolved = self.resolve(subcommand, request_id.clone())?;\n156:          let outcome =\n157:              self.run_resolved(subcommand, &resolved, request, envs, self.options.timeout)?;\n158: -        *self\n159: -            .last_argv\n160: -            .lock()\n161: -            .expect(\"argv mutex should not be poisoned\") = outcome.argv().to_vec();\n162:          let diagnostics = outcome.diagnostics();\n163: -        *self\n164: -            .last_diagnostics\n165: -            .lock()\n166: -            .expect(\"diagnostics mutex should not be poisoned\") = diagnostics.clone();\n167: -        if diagnostics.stdout.truncated {\n168: -            return Err(ProviderClientError::protocol(\n169: -                HostErrorKind::StdoutLimitExceeded,\n170: -                subcommand,\n171: -                request_id,\n172: -                diagnostics,\n173: -            ));\n174: -        }\n175: -        if outcome.stdout.bytes.is_empty() {\n176: -            let kind = if provider_nonzero(&outcome.status) {\n177: -                HostErrorKind::ProviderProcessNonzero\n178: -            } else if outcome.stdin_closed_early && outcome.stderr.bytes.is_empty() {\n179: -                HostErrorKind::ProviderClosedStdinEarly\n180: -            } else {\n181: -                HostErrorKind::EmptyStdout\n182: -            };\n183: -            return Err(ProviderClientError::host_transport(\n184: -                kind,\n185: -                subcommand,\n186: -                request_id,\n187: -                diagnostics,\n188: -            ));\n189: -        }\n190: -        let envelope = parse_one_stdout_object(\n191: +        self.record_process_state(&outcome, &diagnostics);\n192: +        ensure_invocation_stdout_within_limit(subcommand, &diagnostics, request_id.clone())?;\n193: +        ensure_invocation_stdout_present(\n194: +            subcommand,\n195: +            &outcome,\n196: +            diagnostics.clone(),\n197: +            request_id.clone(),\n198: +        )?;\n199: +        let envelope = parse_invocation_stdout_object(\n200:              subcommand,\n201:              &outcome.stdout.bytes,\n202:              &diagnostics,\n203:         